\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - BOJ keeps yen policy, stresses cooperation. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 1999\par 12:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 1 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan portrayed itself as a team player in the government's fight against the high yen on Wednesday, saying its present easy monetary policy also seeks to tame the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , criticised in recent months for opacity and stubbornness in not easing policy further, stepped up its PR offensive, rejecting arguments that it was not doing enough to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  halt the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  It also left open the possibility of further action if the yen surges further. \par \par \uc2  BOJ SUPPORTS GOVT \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  POLICY \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan supports the recent action by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and strongly hopes that market stability is restored as soon as possible," BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said in a rare statement, referring to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday and Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Hayami said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been providing ample funds to the market, but again signalled flexibility in dealing with any further market turmoil. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, he said, was prepared to "prepared to promptly provide sufficient funds" to keep the market stable, "taking account of the government's yen funding of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and any disruptive impact of excessive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on domestic financial transactions." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ since February has driven short-term interest rates essentially to zero by pumping yen into the Tokyo money market as a means to support the tentative economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  It has repeatedly rebuffed pressure, at times overtly applied by the government, to take further, radical easing measures. But minutes of the Policy Board's policy meetings show that the bank was strongly concerned about the high yen even when it refused to change policy earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  The bank released Hayami's statement just moments after it "sterilised" Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mopping up the excess yen left in the banking system from the authorities' yen sales. Instead, the BOJ used its regular morning \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave the same daily fund surplus it has left in the market since May. \par \par \uc2  That move had been widely expected, but some in the markets held out expectations that the BOJ would leave the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilised" in an attempt to weaken the yen's value through oversupply. \par \par \uc2  Hayami's statement briefly caused confusion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and lifted the dollar to 102.55 yen as some traders wondered if he had signalled a further credit easing. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar later slipped back to finish the Tokyo session at around 102.11 after a senior BOJ official effectively quashed speculation of an easing during a briefing with reporters. \par \par \uc2  BOJ REJECTS STERILISED \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 , GOVT APPROVES \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stuck to its position that the whole sterilisation argument is meaningless given the huge surplus the BOJ already leaves in the market to implement its self-styled "zero interest rate policy". \par \par \uc2  "Our monetary policy is more generous that those advocates of so-called unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggest," said Izumi Yamashita, head of the BOJ's financial markets department, who was trotted out for a rare on-the-record briefing for reporters. \par \par \uc2  Hayami's statement was welcomed by the government, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sets \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  It has not always been thus. \par \par \uc2  The ministry publicly but vainly pressured the BOJ to ease policy in this autumn as the yen climbed to a four-year high of 103 to the dollar after investors poured money into yen assets on economic data showing Japan pulling out of its deep recession. \par \par \uc2  The yen has again taken off in recent days, with the dollar falling to 101.25 on Friday and the euro plumbing record lows against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompting this week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen's strength has weighed on stock prices and brought on waves of comments from government officials that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved out of line from economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "As I have said before, an excessive strengthening of the yen is not desirable for the Japanese and world economies," Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda told reporters on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Those hoping the Bank of Japan will adopt an easier approach to policy received a boost on Wednesday after parliament approved the appointment of economist Teizo Taya, a proponent of further easing, to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nine-member Policy Board. \par \par \uc2  Taya, the managing director of the Daiwa Institute of Research, will replace member Yasuo Gotoh who retired in October. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvc108zk6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
