\par\par \b \uc2  World Markets: Shares Rise In Europe, Latin America; Tokyo Stocks Slip \b0\par\par\uc2 1,096 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 1999\par 06:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Shares in Europe finished higher Wednesday, as did stock markets in Latin America and Toronto. Asian markets ended mixed, with Tokyo's key index slipping amid uncertainties about the Bank of Japan's monetary policy, but Hong Kong shares edging higher. \par \par \uc2  London's FT-SE 100 index rose 48.8 points, or 0.7%, to close at 6646.0 after falling 1.4% on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Technical factors, portfolio window-dressing and corporate activity should help the United Kingdom market climb further, said Richard Batty, HSBC global strategist. A senior trader at a French bank added, "There's lots of M&A (merger and acquisition) activity around at the moment. Levels aren't prepared to slow down just yet." \par \par \uc2  Frankfurt's blue-chip DAX index rose 37.80 points, or 0.6%, to close at 5933.84, after edging up 7.16 points Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Shares of heavyweight Deutsche Telekom slid just before the market closed to end down 65 cents, or 1.1%, at 56.65 euros. The shares had traded higher after Morgan Stanley Dean Witter and Credit Suisse First Boston increased their price targets for the shares to 66 euros and 63 euros, respectively. The analysts said Telekom's third-quarter results came in above expectations and it has good potential to increase its earnings before interest and taxes. \par \par \uc2  In Paris, the CAC-40 index closed up 39.79 points, or 0.7%, at 5381.41, after a 0.6% decline Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Shares of French pay-television group Canal Plus climbed 4.70 euros, or 5.7%, to 86.80 euros after a day of turbulent trading. Investors returned to the stock in flurries of bargain-hunting thanks to the lure of its pay-TV software business, which traders said seems undervalued. \par \par \uc2  The stock had risen 29% over two days at the end of last week after the success of U.S. software company OpenTV's Nasdaq listing, which gave investors a yardstick to measure Canal Plus's software assets. But Canal Plus fell sharply Monday and Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  As reported earlier Wednesday, Canal Plus said it will turn its technology division into a separate unit this year, with the aim of spinning it off in some way next year. \par \par \uc2  In Moscow, the benchmark RTS Index rose 1.05 points, or 0.9%, to 113.41, as the trading volume dropped to $8.74 million from $17.62 million Tuesday. Traders said the market was volatile, as overseas investors kept to the sidelines on concerns about Russia's talks with the London Club of creditors, the fighting in Chechnya and upcoming parliamentary elections. \par \par \uc2  Among other European markets, Zurich's Swiss Market index rose 78.70 points, or 1.1%; Madrid's Ibex index rose 126.2 points, or 1.2%; Milan's MIBtel index gained 250 points, or 1.0%; Amsterdam's AEX index edged up 0.46 point, or 0.1%, and Stockholm's Swedish General index rose 92.13 points, or 2.0%. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Stoxx Index of 592 European companies closed up 2.96 points, or 0.9%, at 342.13, while the Dow Jones Euro Stoxx Index, which tracks 297 companies in countries in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ended 2.19 points higher at 363.23. \par \par \uc2  In Latin America, Mexico City's IPC index rose 64.67 points, or 1.1%, to 7592.50 after falling 2.6% Tuesday. Mexican stocks recovered from the previous session's profit-taking, supported by gains in U.S. equities. \par \par \uc2  On Wall Street, the Dow Jones Industrial Average jumped 120.58 points, or 1.1%, to 10998.39. \par \par \uc2  In Argentina, the key Merval index added 7.40 points, or 1.4%, to 543.51 after sliding 2.4% Tuesday. "Significant gains on Wall Street provided underlying support," one Buenos Aires trader said, but trading was light. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's benchmark Bovespa index closed up 95.45 points, or 0.7%, at 13874.31 after falling 0.6% Tuesday. Local investors snapped up shares late in the session after two days of profit-taking, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In Canada, Toronto's TSE 300 index gained 67.08 points, or 0.9%, to 7592.50 after skidding 3.3% Tuesday, helped by strength in U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  Heavyweights Nortel Networks and BCE both climbed, supporting the TSE 300's rise. BCE, Canada's largest telecommunications company, added 3.40 Canadian dollars to C$102.40, while networking firm Nortel gained C$2.30 to C$110.80. Both companies declined about 8% on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Tokyo, the blue-chip Nikkei 225 index fell 62.28 points, or 0.3%, to close at 18495.95, extending Tuesday's 292.04-point loss. However, advancing stocks beat decliners by 7 to 6 on volume of 710.1 million shares. \par \par \uc2  "Investors took profits on recent gainers amid a lack of direction in the overall market, and they used the proceeds to buy laggards," said a trader at a Tokyo brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market also pressured the stock market after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent confusing signals about whether it had sterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts earlier this week. In an unusual statement Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggested it was changing policy by leaving yen money markets unsterilized after its back-to-back dollar-buying sprees Monday and Tuesday. With unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ would leave the extra yen supply resulting from its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system, which in effect would lower the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value. \par \par \uc2  Later Wednesday, the BOJ issued another statement clarifying the first, and officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and BOJ seemed to be contradicting themselves in trying to explain whether the bank's monetary policy had changed. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng index rose 45.33 points, or 0.3%, to close at 15422.52, erasing some of Tuesday's 83.92-point loss. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip index was buoyed by gains in Hutchison Whampoa and its parent Cheung Kong, on market talk it is about to sell a stake in its telecom unit to Japan's NTT DoCoMo. Hutchison Whampoa shares gained 2.6%, while Cheung Kong jumped 3.4%. \par \par \uc2  Buying of second-tier and small technology stocks that have lagged behind in recent sessions also helped to support the market. \par \par \uc2  Highlighting other Asian stock markets, Singapore's main index fell 1.3%, while Indonesian shares climbed 2.3% and Taiwan's main stock index rose 0.6%. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dvc100jkk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
