\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Watch: Extraordinary PR Effort Rebuffed By Analysts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Takeshi Takeuchi \par \uc2 963 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 1999\par 05:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- In an extraordinary sequence of events, the Bank of Japan tried Wednesday to convince the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that it has been taking drastic steps to ease upward pressure on the yen, but analysts dismissed the move as a failed public relations exercise. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami issued a statement at the start of the day, just as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was conducting daily money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying, "The Bank of Japan supports the recent action by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and strongly hopes that market stability is restored as soon as possible." \par \par \uc2  The statement also said the BOJ is ready to supply ample funds to the market, "taking account of the government's yen funding of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and any disruptive impact of excessive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on domestic financial transactions. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank has been flexibly providing ample funds to the short-term money market taking account of factors including yen liquidity arising from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and, under this \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  framework, today continued to supply substantial amount of liquidity," the statement said. \par \par \uc2  The statement was an attempt to persuade market participants that the BOJ isn't undermining the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The crux of the issue is whether the BOJ is "sterilizing" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds by absorbing excess yen sold during \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Such a step, in theory, would negate the longer-term impact of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders watch the figure for excess reserves for clues as to whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expanding policy. Wednesday morning, the BOJ left the figure at Y1 trillion - as it has done for months. \par \par \uc2  In two briefings aimed at explaining Hayami's statement, a BOJ official said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds unsterilized and the market shouldn't look to the reserve figure for policy signals. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were skeptical, however. \par \par \uc2  Hiroshi Saito, general manager of the international treasury division at Mitsubishi Trust & Banking, said Wednesday, "If the BOJ wanted to emphasize that they were avoiding or were stopping their sterilization policy, they probably would have put Y1 trillion plus some money into the market this morning." \par \par \uc2  (Corrected 0722GMT) \par \par \uc2  Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo, said the BOJ was reverting to an "Orwellian" use of language. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, they're maintaining the same excess liquidity...A yen is a yen. It doesn't matter where it's coming from. The bottom line is how much they're leaving in the money market," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the initial confusion following the release of the statement, the dollar edged higher. That move was spurred when Japanese broadcaster NHK reported that the statement essentially signalled a further easing. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to an intraday high of Y102.55 before falling back after comments emerging from the BOJ briefings belied NHK's interpretation. In early afternoon trading in Tokyo, the dollar was trading around Y102.20, not far off its intraday low of Y102.05. \par \par \uc2  The events Wednesday showed that despite recent efforts by the BOJ to bolster transparency, there remains a gap in understanding between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the private sector. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ official briefing reporters admitted as much in his comments. \par \par \uc2  Izumi Yamashita, head of the BOJ's financial markets department, said that analysts and traders don't seem to understand the concept of leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds unsterilized. \par \par \uc2  "Proponents (say) unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help curb the yen's rise because that type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would provide more money to the market. But this notion is not right under the zero rate policy. In fact, the current policy is virtually providing more funds to the market than unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Yamashita said. \par \par \uc2  He added: "Money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are now carried out in such a way as to avoid a yen rise or help lower the yen. Although we left Y1 trillion in excess reserves after the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, it doesn't mean the BOJ has sterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday." \par \par \uc2  Whether the BOJ's explanation made sense or not, some said it could have been intended to pave the way for joint dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by other Group of Seven (G7) countries. \par \par \uc2  If nothing more, the statement was at least an attempt to show that the BOJ and MOF are on the same page in trying to curb the yen rise. Cooperation between Japan's fiscal and monetary authorities is a key condition set at the last G7 meeting in Washington before other G7 nations will consider joining \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts, they noted. \par \par \uc2  "The statement of the last G7 statement called on the BOJ to consider \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in making policy. That's the basic condition for cooperation from the G7. The issuance of such a statement could give the appearance that the BOJ is acting in line with the accord agreed to in that meeting," said Yasunari Ueno, senior economist at Fuji Securities. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials welcomed the BOJ's statement after it was released. \par \par \uc2  Still, others said it seemed highly unlikely that such apparently confusing signals would finally convince \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's G-7 partners to join in coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially since officials overseas aren't likely to believe that the BOJ has done all it can to halt the yen's climb. \par \par \uc2  -By Takeshi Takeuchi (813-5255-2929;ttakeuchi@ap.org) \par \par \uc2  Hiroshi Saito, general manager of the international treasury division at Mitsubishi Trust & Banking, said Wednesday, "If the BOJ wanted to emphasize that they were avoiding or were stopping their sterilization policy, they probably would have put Y1 trillion plus some money into the market this morning." \par \par \uc2  (An item timed around 0425 GMT mis-represented the current policy.) \par \par \uc2  1042 4131 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvc1088fd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
