\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\uc2  For 2nd Day In Bid To Weaken Yen's Value \b0\par\par\uc2 898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 1999\par 07:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the second straight day Tuesday in another attempt to curb the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. The BOJ also bought dollars for yen Monday in an effort to stop the yen's recent surge and help Japan's burgeoning economic recovery, but both attempts had only limited results. \par \par \uc2  Japan is fighting to weaken the yen's value because a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes the country's exports less competitive and erodes manufacturers' overseas earnings, potentially undercutting one of the pillars of Japan's nascent economic recovery. Despite the BOJ's action, however, the dollar edged lower in Asian and U.S. dealings Tuesday. Traders deem success in curbing the yen's appreciation against the dollar unlikely unless the U.S. and European governments aid Tokyo, something they have shown little inclination to do so far. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen around 1:25 a.m. EST through Japanese trust banks. Minutes later, the dollar was quoted at 102.60 yen, up from 102.37 yen late Monday in New York. However, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  settled back down to 101.80 yen in Asia and hit a fresh four-year low of 101.34 yen in Europe before recovering in later New York trading. Late Tuesday in New York, the dollar was quoted at 102.10 yen, down from 102.37 yen late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Still many market players don't have much faith in the Bank of Japan's latest efforts to stop the yen's advance. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs, confirmed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Speaking to reporters after meeting with Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, Kuroda said: "The yen's strengthening since last week was abnormal. There was fear that a rapid strengthening in the yen's value would have a negative effect on the economy." \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar's brief drift higher in Asia from its New York level on short-covering, yen-buying gained momentum as investors bid up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on expectations of an economic recovery in Japan and higher stock prices in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Tuesday that Japan would seek cooperation from other Group of Seven industrialized nations, if necessary, to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. "Japan is not satisfied with current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels," Miyazawa told a regular news conference. "I can't tell whether G-7 cooperation is necessary now to address the situation, but we are ready to ask for their cooperation if necessary," Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japan's former vice finance minister for international affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said Tuesday that in order to prevent an excessive future strengthening of the yen, monetary policy steps must be combined with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \par \par \uc2  Speaking at a tourism convention, Sakakibara, who became known as "Mr. Yen" for his strong influence on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, said that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Japan cooperated, the U.S. would join in support of the dollar as well. \par \par \uc2  "I hope the BOJ and MOF will cooperate very closely to avoid an excessive and premature yen appreciation. If the BOJ and MOF cooperate, I think (U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence) Summers will cooperate as well," he said. \par \par \uc2  Summers renewed his commitment Monday to the so-called strong-dollar policy, saying: "A strong dollar is in our national interest." \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government finds itself battling a burst of optimism over Japan's economic prospects, The Wall Street Journal reported Tuesday. One of the main forces behind the yen's strength is heavy buying of Japanese shares -- and hence the yen needed to purchase them -- by foreign investors who are encouraged by signs of an economic recovery. Overseas investors were the most active buyers of stocks on Japanese exchanges through the first three weeks of November, a period in which they bought 666 billion yen ($6.55 billion) in Japanese shares. \par \par \uc2  Foreign multinationals also are snapping up stakes in Japanese companies, as the country opens itself wider than ever to outside investment, notably in finance and telecommunications. Foreign direct investment in Japan hit a record 1.34 trillion yen in the year ended in March -- a quarter of what Japanese companies invested overseas that year, but still a 98% rise from the previous year. \par \par \uc2  Optimism over Japan's economy has persisted in spite of the yen, which began climbing from the 120 yen level this summer. Not long ago, many observers forecast Japan's economy would shrink next year, but growth predictions are rising. Liberal Democratic Party policy chief, Shizuka Kamei, said Sunday he expects gross domestic product could grow by "at least 1.6%" in the year starting April 1. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, there is some evidence that Japanese companies can withstand this bout of yen strength -- as long as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't soar, as it did in 1995, when it touched 79 yen per dollar. In a survey based on Japanese companies' earnings forecasts, the business daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported Saturday that the aggregate pretax profit of listed nonfinancial companies is expected to climb 11% in the year ending March 2000 from a year earlier, the Journal noted. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dvbu00ldh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
