\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS - Factors to stall yen rise in short supply. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 1999\par 08:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 30 (Reuters) - A sharp slide in Tokyo shares, disappointing Japanese economic data, joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the world's leading economies or a credit easing by the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Any of these could stall the yen's rise, but the prospects for such scenarios seem slim - for now. \par \par \uc2  A break of the yen beyond 100 to the dollar "looks pretty likely unless someone pulls something out of their hat, like joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that seems pretty unlikely," said Peter Morgan, economist at HSBC Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose as high as 101.35 per dollar in Tokyo on Tuesday but fell back to 102.30 on rumours of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  STOCKS HOLD UP, BUT FOR HOW LONG? \par \par \uc2  Fears of damage to Japan's emerging economic recovery from a robust yen could jolt stock market investors and send share prices tumbling, but so far the market has held up pretty well. \par \par \uc2  That's because much of the appetite - among foreign investors especially - is for so-called "New Japan" firms that are expected to benefit from a domestic boom in information and technology or are restructuring aggressively to boost profitability. \par \par \uc2  "There's a very virtuous cycle at work. A lot of fund managers are concentrating their holdings on a lot of sexy stocks and these keep attracting new money," said Chris Calderwood, chief economist at Jardine Fleming in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The 'New Japan' is relatively yen insensitive." \par \par \uc2  Tokyo share prices weakened on Tuesday, however, as the yen's firmness dampened enthusiasm for exporters, dragging the Nikkei share average down 1.55 percent to 18,558.23 by the close. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the downside was limited by expectations new investment trusts would plunk money into info-tech shares. \par \par \uc2  Still, optimism in the face of a relentlessly strong yen could well fade, in turn easing pressure on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "At the end of the day, stocks respond to profits...and a strong yen can deal a blow to the market," said Russell Jones, chief economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  MACRO-SURPRISES POSSIBLE \par \par \uc2  Close attention, meanwhile, will be given to gross domestic product (GDP) data for the July-September quarter due to be released on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Economists warned that the very volatilty of GDP data and difficulties predicting it mean that numbers could easily fall outside the range of expectations. An upside shock would bolster the yen while disappointing data would undermine it. \par \par \uc2  A recent Reuters poll yielded an average forecast of a 0.1 percent decline in the latest quarter after surprisingly strong growth in the first half of the year. \par \par \uc2  Some economists pointed to hints of economic weakness from data released on Tuesday, including a 2.4 percent fall in spending by wage earners' households in October from a year earlier and an 18.1 percent drop in orders received by major Japanese construction firms in the same month. \par \par \uc2  "This doesn't catch attention immediately, it has to filter in," said Robert Feldman, chief economist for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Morgan Stanley Dean Witter. \par \par \uc2  CREDIT EASING UNLIKELY, JOINT \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  A LONG SHOT \par \par \uc2  A move by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) to ease its already hyper-loose zero interest rate policy by adopting quantitative targets or increasing its outright purchases of government bonds could also put the brakes on the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  But several economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to wait and see whether the yen's rise actually hurts the economy - which can also reap benefits from cheaper imports due to a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - before taking any such drastic step. \par \par \uc2  "It's always a card up their sleeve," Morgan said. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is probably the single biggest worry at this point, that's clearly stated in the (BOJ) minutes. If the yen went to 95 (to the dollar) maybe that would prompt them to do some sort of quantitative easing," he added. \par \par \uc2  Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise is also a long shot, given few signs that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to rescue the euro or that the United States is worried about the dollar, still strong against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would send a very strong signal to the market," Calderwood said. "Unfortunately, I can't see how or why the U.S. would be tempted to join in, not quickly." ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432 8018 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbu095bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
