\par\par \b \uc2  Miyazawa says not satisfied with current  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  levels. \b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 1999\par 03:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 30 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Tuesday he was not satisfied with current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  Asked for his view on the yen's strength against the dollar, even after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Monday, Miyazawa said he wanted to monitor current trends in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a while. \par \par \uc2  He told a regular news conference: "I would like to hear the opinions of my staff, but I am not satisfied with the current levels." \par \par \uc2  He added that the situation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is difficult, as it is possible that dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could create an opportunity for traders who want to sell dollars to do so. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said he told his staff on Monday evening that they should make decisions on how to deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after looking at Tokyo trading on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  "I will go back to the ministry after this and take a look at the situation," he told a news conference held in parliament. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether he thinks concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is unnecessary, Miyazawa said: "I am not saying that." \par \par \uc2  He added that Haruhiko Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, was always in touch with the U.S. and European authorities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Regarding the euro, Miyazawa said he has not heard many calls from European authorities for the euro to be supported before it hits parity with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "As a whole, European authorities do not think (current levels of) the euro are problematic." \par \par \uc2  Asked whether there were differences between Japan and European authorities on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Miyazawa said: "I am not saying that there are differences." \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said, however, he has not heard of any talks between \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and European central bankers on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the euro's behalf. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) have prepared to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the euro twice in the past, but they ended up not conducting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at those occasions, he added. \par \par \uc2  "At that time, when we asked them 'Should we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the euro?' they said 'Good'. But I have not heard any such talk this time." \par \par \uc2  Regarding candidates for the next head of the International Monetary Fund, Miyazawa said he had former Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara in mind, but added that Japan has not made any final decision on the matter. \par \par \uc2  He added that Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi had asked China and South Korea to support a possible Japanese candidate as head of the IMF at their summit meeting held in Manila on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  ((Yoko Nishikawa, Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbu094q0\par }\page {\