\par\par \b \uc2  Euro spikes up vs dollar, led by gains vs sterling. \b0\par\par\uc2 246 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 1999\par 11:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 30 (Reuters) - The euro leapt more than a quarter of a cent against the dollar on Tuesday, its rise away from parity led by sudden gains it racked up against sterling, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said rumours were swirling around the foreign exchanges that purchases of euros for pounds were being conducted on behalf of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  but they added that no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been detected so far. \par \par \uc2  Such rumours were sparked as several German banks were seen buying euros around 63.10 pence and until the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached levels around 63.30. British banks were also said to be buying the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for pounds. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England declined to comment on sterling's move and the talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose as high as $1.0144 by 1020 GMT according to Reuters data, more than a cent above the life lows it plumbed on Monday. Euro/sterling rose to peaks of 63.41 pence, up from the life low of 62.62 pence it hit last week. \par \par \uc2  Traders have been on alert for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during European hours after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in more than two months on Monday and sold yen again in the foreign exchanges during Asian trading on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Swaha Pattanaik, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbu094im