\par\par \b \uc2  JGBs rise on strong note auction but gains blocked. \b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 1999\par 09:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 30 (Reuters) - The key March 10-year Japanese government bond futures extended gains by Tokyo close on Tuesday as a two-year note auction produced healthy results, but dealers said the market lacked the energy to climb further. \par \par \uc2  The yen's strength and falling share prices also provided support to JGB prices. \par \par \uc2  "There were plenty of reasons to expect the futures to rise further, but the market turned cautious ahead of Thursday's (four-year note) auction," a Japanese city bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said an auction of 700 billion yen in 0.5 percent two-year government notes produced a lowest accepted price of 100.10 and an average price of 100.12. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at the short tail, it was a good auction. But the market was already pretty long in the futures market before the results came out," a trust bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  The March futures ended the Tokyo session at 130.95, up 0.38 from Monday's close. The contract moved in a range of 130.68 to 131.14. \par \par \uc2  At 0742 GMT, the yield on the 218th 10-year cash JGB stood at 1.830 percent, down 0.005 from Monday. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment brightened on evidence of buying of medium-term bonds, prices of which had dropped due to expectations for Japan's economic recovery and additional bond issuances. \par \par \uc2  Some investors, possibly including government-affiliated financial institutions, continued to shift into long bonds from medium-term bonds. But other investors showed interest in selling long bonds into rallies, as they now expect the middle of the curve to outperform the long end. \par \par \uc2  The yen's surge in value continued to prompt buying of Japanese bonds. The yen firmed to an intraday high of 101.33 to the dollar before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling yen for a second successive day, stopping the Japanese unit's rally at least temporarily, traders said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0805 GMT, the yen stood at 102.15/20 yen. \par \par \uc2  Demand for yen bonds by foreign investors may increase if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to appreciate against the dollar, as that may prompt fund managers to increase the proportion of JGBs in their global portfolios. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign portfolio managers had been rapidly increasing their weightings in JGBs over the past couple of months. The yen's recent trend could fuel more buying," a brokerage trader said. \par \par \uc2  But others were wary of such views. \par \par \uc2  "If it's the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I'm a bit sceptical... The strong performance of the U.S. economy and the Japanese economic recovery scenario have been pretty much priced into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's really the euro-zone recovery that has not been priced in yet," another brokerage trader said. \par \par \uc2  In the money markets, key June three-month TIBOR-based euroyen futures settled at 99.695, from 99.660 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan continued to leave the money market with a net surplus of one trillion yen after its regular 0020 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Settlements of the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday will occur on Wednesday. If the BOJ leaves the net surplus in the money market unchanged, it will indicate that it has sterilised - or drained excess yen funds created by - the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The key overnight call rate was mainly traded at around 0.02 to 0.03 percent, unchanged from Monday's weighted average. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry said on Tuesday it would offer 2.2 trillion yen worth of six-month Treasury bills via auction on December 7. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 1396 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbu0939b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
