\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Euro near lows, Euro stocks slide, oil down. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tim Castle \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 1999\par 16:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 30 (Reuters) - The euro held just above record lows against the dollar and the psychological $1.00 level in volatile trade on Tuesday, with swings exaggerated by persistent talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following another bout of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen sales in Asia. \par \par \uc2  European stock markets fell sharply on profit-taking and bonds moved higher, with energy shares depressed after crude oil prices fell back on news that Iraq was preparing to resume exports. \par \par \uc2  U.S. bond yields briefly rose on higher-than-expected purchasing managers' data. Wall Street stocks opened mixed. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \tab \tab \tab      MARKET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  PRICES AT 1534 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   EURO  1.0071/75\tab  YEN  101.86/94\tab   STERLING  1.5946/56\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   GOLD  $291.30/292.30 +0.44 (pvs PM fix)  BRENT  $24.34 -0.51\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   FTSE  6607.2 -85.10  CAC 5,344.85 -29.06  DAX 5879.93 -8.95\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Earlier in a choppy session the euro racked up around a quarter of a cent against the dollar and jumped against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid rumours that euros were being purchased for pounds on behalf of a European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , possibly the Bundesbank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It retreated again after German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder and French President Jacques Chirac said they were not worried about prevailing euro exchange rates, but market nervousness about the central banks remained.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell as far as $1.0045, the low for the day and within striking distance of the $1.0034 life low it hit on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro also slid against the yen, trading less than 0.5 percent away from the all-time low of 102.23 it set on Monday according to Reuters data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asian trade had lifted the dollar to a high of 103 yen from near its four-year low of 101.22 hit on Friday, and assisted the euro up to around 104 yen. By early afternoon the dollar had retreated to below 102 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TELECOMS AND TECHS LEAD WAY DOWN\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European shares lost ground quickly in early trading, as weak U.S. stocks and the shaky euro pushed investors to take profits after recent record highs.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par High valuation sectors such as technology and telecoms were the heaviest losers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Finnish mobile phone group Nokia  was off 5.1 percent, Dutch telecom operator KPN Telecoms NV  was down 3.5 percent and Ericsson  was off 2.9 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Energy stocks slid on lower oil prices, with BP Amoco  down 1.3 percent and France's Elf-Aquitaine  down 1.4 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shares bucking the downward trend included British hi-tech audio firm NXT Plc , whose shares rocketed 60 percent on news that it had agreed an $8 billion joint venture with the UK government.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 1514 GMT the pan-European Eurotop index of 300 shares was down 19.56 points or 1.35 percent at 1434.56 while the narrower Euro Stoxx index of 50 blue chip euro zone shares was down 61.16 points or 1.40 percent at 4303.16.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among leading European blue-chip bourse indices, London's FTSE 100 was down 1.5 percent, Frankfurt's DAX was down 0.6 percent, and Paris's CAC was down 0.9 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Wall Street the Dow Industrial Average was up 29.06 points or 0.26 percent at 10,976.67 at 1514 GMT. The technology-heavy Nasdaq was down 46.93 points or 1.37 percent at 3,374.44.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Oil prices fell sharply as key producer Iraq started to prepare for a resumption of its oil exports.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 1513 GMT the January Brent crude oil future was down 57 cents at $24.28, off a low of $24.22.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. bond yields blipped higher after National Association of Purchasing Management (NAPM)-Chicago data showed its prices paid index jumped to 70.9 in November from 65.4 in October.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The long bond was trading at 97 19/32, up 2/32 from New York's close. Its yield stood at 6.304 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The December Bund future was up 0.24 points at 105.62, after reaching a high of 105.69. The benchmark 10-year cash Bund was yielding 5.192 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbu08yc5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
