\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Soars In Asia After Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\uc2  To Curb Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 1,242 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 06:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar briefly surged above 104 yen Monday in Asia after Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  ordered the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to halt the yen's rapid surge and restore stability in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading. \par \par \uc2  Japan doesn't want a stronger yen eroding the value of its exporters' overseas profits, in turn hurting business and consumer confidence in the country's nascent economic recovery, experts said. A stronger yen hurts Japanese exporters -- the main engine of the economy -- by making their products less competitive. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar's abrupt fall last Friday to a four-year low of 101.25 yen in New York, Japan's Finance Ministry and the Bank of Japan wasted no time. Shortly after the release early Monday of worse-than-expected Japanese industrial production data for October, which showed 2.3% decline after a 0.9% fall had been forecast, the Bank of Japan began buying dollars. It bought at first through Japanese city banks and then through foreign banks, dealers said. The volume of Bank of Japan buying totaled more than $1 billion, dealers said. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 14. Later in the session, the Bank of Japan apparently bought euros for yen, according to a Nikkei News report. \par \par \uc2  Still, there were no signs that other central banks were working with the Bank of Japan in its attempts to stem the yen's ascent, and some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders doubted the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would fully reverse the yen's gains without signs of a coordinated effort with the European and U.S. central banks. \par \par \uc2  Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely expected since last week, when Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he had ordered officials to take action as needed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  triggered Japanese and overseas interbank players' short-covering purchase of dollars, sending to it an intraday high of 104.08 yen. \par \par \uc2  In late Asian trading, the dollar was quoted at 103.35 yen, above 101.58 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.0115, lower than $1.0161 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Market participants said that without Bank of Japan action, the dollar would likely have headed the other way. "The dollar was likely to drop below 100 yen if left to the market forces. The MOF and Bank of Japan can't let it go below 100 yen to ensure an economic recovery," said Minoru Shioiri, assistant manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Nippon Credit Bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, however, met strong resistance around 104 yen with sales by Japanese exporters and life-insurance companies as well as overseas investors, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Overseas investors are growing more bullish about the yen and Japanese economy. They will actively sell dollars around 104 yen," said Shuji Takano, assistant manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ABN AMRO Bank. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Miyazawa confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the abnormal rise of the yen," Miyazawa told reporters at his ministry. \par \par \uc2  Japan's chief cabinet secretary Mikio Aoki explained why the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "There is fear that excessive yen strength could hurt an economic recovery, and we have taken appropriate measures," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have repeatedly said the rapid rise in the yen's value against the dollar could hamper the nation's economic recovery from its worst slowdown in decades. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economy has been sluggish since the end of the 1980s, when unbridled speculation left banks and corporations saddled with huge debts. Fears of being laid off have caused workers to squirrel away savings more than ever before, and banks have stopped lending as they struggle to write off bad loans. Before the growth rush that started with the beginning of 1999, Japan's economy had been in a two-year tailspin - the country's worst recession since the end of World War II. Japan's economy seems to be on the road to recovery with recent positive gross domestic product data. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, officials from the Group of Seven industrialized countries also are concerned that the rising yen could threaten the tentative economic recovery in the world's second-largest economy, by exacerbating deflationary pressures and crimping corporate profits, Monday's Wall Street Journal reported. \par \par \uc2  The yen's volatility threatens to unravel the G-7's efforts from September to curb the then-surging Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Officials are hoping Asian markets will return to their calmer patterns of the last two months, without a further rise in the yen, but they are braced for more volatility. \par \par \uc2  The euro's weakness, though unsettling to European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, so far raises less alarm. With European stock markets strong -- France's CAC index soared last week to a record -- officials don't view the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness as a lack of confidence in the European economy. In fact, a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help exports, a possible boon to Germany, Europe's largest economy, which suffers from sluggish domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  Officials were worried by the speed at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were moving and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements weren't easily explained by underlying economic forces. The persistent strength of the U.S. economy, and the prospect of further Federal Reserve rate increases, should tend to boost the dollar against the yen, not weaken it. Similarly, the European economy has been improving, raising expectations of higher interest rates -- also factors that should support the euro. \par \par \uc2  The G-7 (which includes the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, England, Italy and Canada) is more likely to intercede when markets are, as officials put it, "disorderly" or behaving in ways inconsistent with economic fundamentals, either through statements aimed at influencing market participants or by buying or selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In their last go-around with the markets, in September, G-7 finance ministers and central bankers said they "shared Japan's concern about the potential impact of the yen's appreciation for the Japanese economy and the world economy" and "welcomed indications by the Japanese authorities that policies would be conducted appropriately in view of this potential impact." That kept the yen under control for the last two months. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said Japan "took appropriate actions. MOF was afraid that the yen's rise since last week will have bad effects on the Japanese economy." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are less worrisome for the U.S. economy. Officials have watched the dollar's erosion against the yen with some concern in recent months, fearing a weakened dollar could spark American inflation and shake the stock market by making the U.S. assets less attractive for global investors. But the dollar's rise against the euro means that, on a world-wide basis, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained relatively stable. \par \par \uc2  For now, European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials aren't showing much worry, either. They have said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't their primary focus, but is important only because of its effects on the economy. They have made clear their distaste for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets -- at least in part because an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that failed to lift the euro would damage the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility. But they haven't flatly ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , either. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dvbt00k77\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
