\par\par \b \uc2  World Markets: European Shares End Mixed; Latin American Markets Fall \b0\par\par\uc2 977 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 06:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Shares in Europe ended mixed Monday following a similar performance in Asian stock markets. Meanwhile, stocks in Toronto and major Latin American markets followed Wall Street lower. \par \par \uc2  European stock markets have been "quickly swallowed up into the jaws of euro volatility," said Dave Brown, chief European economist at Bear Stearns. The euro-zone \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped closer to parity with the dollar, after setting fresh lows against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yen. \par \par \uc2  Brown said he is expecting "edgy trading" in the next few weeks, with volumes thin ahead of Y2K fears. "But the European stock market will be back with a bang in the new year - the fundamentals are very positive," the economist added. \par \par \uc2  London's FT-SE 100 index rose 7.5 points, or 0.1%, to end at a record 6692.3 after gaining two points to a record on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Investors focused on Royal Bank of Scotland's hostile bid for National Westminster Bank. Royal Bank's offer of 26.5 billion pounds ($42.59 billion) in shares and loan notes was above Bank of Scotland's earlier hostile offer for NatWest of 24.3 billion pounds, but not enough to knock out the competition or to force an immediate NatWest capitulation, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Movements in share prices keep the offer price, and therefore the bid situation, in a state of flux, although NatWest is sure to lose its independence to one of the suitors, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  A trader at a U.S. brokerage said Royal Bank's offer "wasn't the knockout bid they (investors) were looking for". \par \par \uc2  Royal Bank of Scotland fell 87 pence to 1,241 pence, NatWest declined 74 pence to 1,444 pence and Bank of Scotland slid 4.5 pence to 728.5 pence. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Frankfurt's blue-chip DAX index fell 69.19 points, or 1.2%, to end at 5888.88, after slipping 3.38 points on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Shares of Deutsche Telekom pulled the blue-chip index lower after Telecom Italia dismissed rumors TI would sell the German company a stake in its mobile-phone unit, traders said. Deutsche Telekom shares closed down 1.70 euros at 57.00 euros. \par \par \uc2  In Paris, the CAC-40 index fell 23.08 points, or 0.4%, to finish at 5373.91, after Friday's 0.8% rise to a record high. \par \par \uc2  In Moscow, the benchmark RTS Index fell 3.11 points, or 2.6%, to 116.64, as trading volume tumbled to $5.97 million from $17 million Friday. \par \par \uc2  The decline came two days after International Monetary Fund Managing Director Michel Camdessus told a Madrid investor conference that growing concern over Russian human-rights abuses in the rebel republic of Chechnya could hold up the next $640 million installment in Russia's $4.5 billion IMF-loan program. \par \par \uc2  Among other European markets, Amsterdam's AEX index added 1.08 points, or 0.2%, to a record; Zurich's Swiss Market index gained 40.20 points, or 0.6%; Milan's MIBtel index rose 330 points, or 1.3%, and Stockholm's Swedish General index rose 33.59 points, or 0.7%. But Madrid's Ibex index fell 78.8 points, or 0.7%. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Stoxx Index of 600 European companies closed up 0.61 points, or 0.2%, at 345.71 points. The Dow Jones Euro Stoxx Index, which tracks 310 companies in countries that joined the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ended down 0.30 points at 366.00. \par \par \uc2  Major markets in Latin America tracked the Dow Jones Industrial Average lower. On Wall Street, the blue-chip indicator fell 40.99 points, or 0.4%, to 10947.92. \par \par \uc2  Mexico City's key IPC index fell 34.35 points, or 0.5%, to 6303.10 after gaining 0.2% to a record Friday. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's benchmark Bovespa index closed down 33.28 points, or 0.2%, at 13854.98 after gaining 2.6% Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Argentina, the key Merval index closed off 6.14 points, or 1.1%, at 549.30, erasing Friday's 1.1% rise. \par \par \uc2  In Canada, Toronto's TSE 300 index closed down 117.86 points, or 1.5%, at 7772.08 after rising 0.5% to a record Friday. The market was pulled down by a solid decline in the gold group in response to a fall in the price of gold. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Tokyo, the Nikkei 225 index slid 64.23 points, or 0.3%, to close at 18850.27 after Friday's 192.72-point gain, amid renewed concerns that the yen's recent strengthening against the dollar will lower profits for Japan's exporters. Declining stocks beat advancers by 7 to 5 on volume of 561.7 million shares. However, other key indexes closed higher. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei recovered from its intraday lows after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surge against the dollar. Japanese officials have repeatedly said the rapid rise of the yen against the dollar could hamper the nation's recovery from its worst economic slowdown in decades. A stronger yen makes Japanese exports more expensive - and thus less competitive - abroad, while reducing the value of repatriated profits in terms of the yen. \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, shares rose as stronger-than-expected economic growth in the third quarter buoyed investor sentiment, analysts said. The benchmark Hang Seng index rose 186.58 points, or 1.2%, to close at 15461.11, adding to Friday's 1.8% jump. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong late Friday announced 4.5% third-quarter GDP growth, beating the median market estimate of 3% to 3.5%. Traders also attributed the market rally to the rate-setting banking association's decision to leave Hong Kong deposit interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dvbt00k74\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
