\par\par \b \uc2  Miyazawa: Euro Weakness One Reason For Rapid Yen Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 04:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday morning to smooth out disruptive moves, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Monday. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters at the ministry, Miyazawa said, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tool to smooth out disruptive moves in the market." \par \par \uc2  Asked about a view that the yen will eventually strengthen in line with the expected economic recovery in Japan, Miyazawa said, "That is a matter for the longer-term. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a tool to set long-term views." \par \par \uc2  He also said the recent yen's strengthening was partially due to a drop in the euro's value. \par \par \uc2  "I think the euro's weakness was one of causes" of the yen's strengthening, Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 0325 GMT (11:25 p.m. EST Sunday), the dollar was at Y103.95, up\par from Y101.58 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.0135, down from\par $1.0161 Friday in New York.\par -0- 29/11/99 03-42G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbt088b1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
