\par\par \b \uc2  Officials' Credibility Tested As Yen Surges, Euro Dives \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Casey \par \uc2 951 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 20:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- With both the euro and the yen marching inexorably toward key psychological levels, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are sending a powerful message to governments in Europe and Japan. \par \par \uc2  If there is a coherent theme behind the concurrence of the euro's slide toward dollar-parity and the yen's surge toward the 100-yen level against the dollar it is that markets aren't impressed with the credibility of leadership in either place. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, market participants have reacted negatively to the message, if not the substance, behind the German government's bailout of a construction company and its meddling in a cross-border takeover battle, as well as to some perceived inconsistencies in the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policies toward the euro. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, authorities' attempts to weaken the yen have failed so dramatically that their key weapon, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now seems impotent. With the dollar trading early in New York Monday about one yen below the Y102.50 level at which the Bank of Japan bought dollars just twelve hours previously, the stature of the BOJ as a market-beating force looks diminished. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, the euro's downturn is mostly a result of long-euro positions being systematically unwound by traders who were disappointed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's failure to carry through on earlier promises of recovery. But officials did provided something of a catalyst for the selling right at a time when the euro was in need of some confidence-building. \par \par \uc2  "You had the technicals breaking down, you had positions that were a little overweight euros and a touch underweight yen, and then you add to that leadership signals out of Europe that are not constructive," said Robert Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Signal More Important Than Substance\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Germany's $130 million bailout of construction firm Philipp Holzmann\par AG (G.HOZ) is small change in the context of the entire euro-zone\par economy, and it's not the kind of action that other Group-of-Seven\par countries can say they've never taken. Nor is Chancellor Gerhard\par Schroeder's outspoken opposition to Vodafone Airtouch PLC's (U.VOD)\par hostile bid for Mannesman AG (G.MMN) likely to have a tangible effect,\par given that European Union rules are on Vodafone's side.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets were itching for an excuse to sell euros.\par They found it in the actions of the German government.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If Germany, the largest and most important economy in Europe, can send\par out such a clear anti-market message, analysts said, then the prospects\par for long-term growth on the continent look dull. It suggests, some said,\par that a recent improvement in euro-zone economic data is due to a\par cyclical rebound rather than a structural recovery, and thus that the\par growth premium in favor of the dollar won't disappear soon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It was the signal and the sentiment it created," said Susan Stearns,\par director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the Bank of Montreal in New York. "It\par was what that signal said about the will to reform at a very senior\par level, and I think that is a very negative development."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, the economic news out of Europe seems to many to be out of\par synch with the euro's dire performance.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Business confidence is picking up and, in many parts of the euro-zone,\par growth is strong. Meanwhile, governments are getting some things right,\par even in Germany, where analysts applauded the passage on Friday of a\par deficit-cutting budget for 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the market is relentlessly trading the trend, and in thin year-end\par conditions where participants are unwilling to take aggressive positions\par the euro's slide and the yen's advance seem unstoppable.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Coherent, Clear Policy Needed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This is an environment where financial authorities are sometimes\par inclined to take action, to put markets more in line with\par "fundamentals."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But to succeed, coherent actions are needed, often coordinated jointly\par among G-7 countries, and backed by a clear policy message. '\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Clarity has been lacking in Europe, analysts say. European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg was quoted in one report last week as\par saying the euro's fall below parity wouldn't matter, but elsewhere that\par he was concerned about further weakness undermining public confidence in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Meanwhile, executive board member Eugenio Domingo Solans\par said the ECB "rules out formal or informal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " to support the\par euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This inconsistency reveals the "teething problems" faced by euro-zone\par officials who "struggle to speak with one voice," said the Bank of\par Montreal's Stearns.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It stands in contrast with the U.S., she said, noting that although\par the Federal Reserve Board encompasses a range of member opinions, Fed\par chairman Alan Greenspan has the "imprimatur of being a powerful\par consensus builder."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Japan, the problem has not so much been statements by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials but the way in which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par been conducted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the government may have had little option but to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , to\par back up its repeated assertion that a strong yen was "premature" and to\par offer some hope to Japanese exporters, many analysts questioned why the\par BOJ concentrated on buying dollars for yen. The euro was far more in\par need of support than the dollar, they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think they are going after the wrong cross," said Citibank's\par Sinche. "It's sort of the same old response, while the markets are\par focused on something else."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "had less credibility in and of itself because of how\par it was conducted," he added.\par -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires\par -michael.j.casey@dowjones.com; 201-938-2009\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1049 4215 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbt085cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
