\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  bruises yen vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 795 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 06:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars against yen for the first time in over two months on Monday, helping the dollar recoup some of the sharp losses it suffered late last week. Government officials confirmed the BOJ had taken action to rein in the strong yen after it had surged to four-year highs against the dollar and life highs against the euro in New York trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Movements that disturb the markets are not favourable. We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Tokyo market," said Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa. Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda concurred, saying the ministry had taken appropriate action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "We believed the rapid rise in the yen since Friday would have an adverse effect on Japan's economic recovery," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the BOJ was first detected buying dollars at levels around 103 yen early on Monday morning. The dollar, which fell to a four-year low of 101.25 yen overseas on Friday, stood at 103.22/27 yen as of 0208 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action pushed the dollar as high as 103.90 yen on Monday morning. The euro stood at $1.0151/61 as of 0210 GMT against $1.0164 in late U.S. trading on Friday. Dealers' views on the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  varied, with some giving estimates of $500 million to $1.0 billion and others saying it could have been as much as $2.0 billion. \par \par \uc2  They said the supply-demand balance in the market was still tilted in the yen's favour, with many Japanese exporters and investors eager to sell the dollar at levels near 104 yen to 105 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The potential for further yen strength remains...and the situation is exacerbated by a dearth of speculators" willing to buy dollars, said Kimihiko Tomita, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Chase Manhattan Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The resilience of U.S. asset markets despite lingering concerns about inflation helped push the dollar above 106 yen early last week, but selling led by Japanese exporters quickly dragged the greenback lower, Tomita said. \par \par \uc2  With speculators now forced to sell dollars to cut their losses, there is no one left to absorb Japanese firms' dollar-selling needs except the BOJ, he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yen was also likely to receive a boost from further capital flows into Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Flows of funds into Japan are likely to continue, and I think the trend of yen firmness will remain in place," said Hikari Sekioka, vice president of market research at Sakura Bank. \par \par \uc2  Key economic data, such as Japan's third-quarter gross domestic product due next Monday as well as the Bank of Japan's "tankan" corporate sentiment survey due the following Monday, are seen as possible catalysts for a rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Economic Planning Agency said on Monday it will announce Japan's gross domestic product data for the July-September quarter at 8.50 a.m. on December 6. (2350 GMT December 5). \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think the dollar will trade mostly in a range of 100 yen to 108 yen until the year-end, but a fall below 100 yen can't be ruled out," said a senior trader for a U.S. commercial bank. One of the factors behind the dollar's fall to a four-year low of 101.25 yen\tab   on Friday was yen-buying by U.S. investors for the purpose of purchasing Japanese assets, he said. The dollar was also hurt by selling of euros against yen by Japanese investors and exporters, which pushed the euro to a life low around 102.48 yen\tab      in overseas markets on Friday. Dealers were sceptical of the chances of joint yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the BOJ and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , judging from recent comments by European monetary officials. Jean-Rene Bernard, a member of the Bank of France's Monetary Policy Council, said in Tokyo on Monday that the recent weakness of the euro is not a cause for concern as it is not stoking inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bernard's comments came on the heels of comments by ECB board member Domingo Solans, who said on Saturday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had no plans to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the euro, but left the door open to future action.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not going to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Domingo said, but added: "Later on, if an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does occur, don't blame me."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro stood at 105.28/33 yen as of 0305 GMT, up from 103.28 yen in late U.S. trading on Friday. ((Masayuki Kitano, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbt099uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
