\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS - Euro fall may force ECB to guide mkts more. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tomasz Janowski \par \uc2 808 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 19:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FRANKFURT, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems content to watch on calmly as the euro sinks to new depths daily, but at some point it will need to take some action to give financial markets clues on its thinking, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  While the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets on Monday to halt the yen's rise the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  watched the 11-month-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  make record lows without any attempts to throw the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a lifeline. \par \par \uc2  Parity against the dollar is now less than one cent away. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the ECB's inaction, contrasting with Japan's efforts, showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was neither overly concerned about the economic fallout of the euro's weakness nor convinced an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have a lasting impact. "Fundamentally the weaker euro has a positive effect on growth as it helps exports while on the inflation side it doesn't have so much impact that one should get concerned," said Frank Schroeder, economist at HSBC Trinkaus in Duesseldorf. \par \par \uc2  As the euro hit life lows of $1.0038 and 102.34 yen earlier on Monday, driven down mainly by the yen's strength, the ECB resorted to the familiar game of warning financial markets that they should not assume no action yet meant none to come. \par \par \uc2  ECB President Wim Duisenberg told the European Parliament in Brussels that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained a "potential weapon." The euro recovered to $1.0092 by 1752 GMT. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  COULD BE DOUBLE-EDGED SWORD \par \par \uc2  But analysts warned that the ECB had also to weigh the downside risks of taking direct action in the markets. Failure to turn the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trend by stepping in and buying euros would be a blow to its credibility, they warned. \par \par \uc2  "Japan is a good example that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot hold out against a strong market trend for long," Schroeder said. \par \par \uc2  The ECB itself has sold its hands-off approach by expressing repeated confidence that it is only a matter of time before the euro will rebound without outside help - sending the basic message to markets that they should do their sums again. \par \par \uc2  "Ultimately, there is one way the euro will and shall go, and that is up," ECB President Wim Duisenberg told the European Parliament in Brussels on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The growth gap between the euro zone and the booming U.S. economy, which has been weighing on the euro/dollar exchange rate, should eventually disappear, and in two years the euro zone economic expansion would outstrip U.S. growth, he said. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg also said that weaker euro did not threaten price stability in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area and did not call for a monetary policy response. \par \par \uc2  He added, however, the "hype" surrounding the euro's exchange rate could erode public confidence in the new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  MARKETS SEEK ECB "PAIN LEVEL" \par \par \uc2  But some analysts said the ECB may have no choice but to let markets know where its "pain level" was or face repeated tests of its patience. \par \par \uc2  "I'm bullish on European growth but the ECB's job now is to bridge the gap betwen the long term potential for the euro and short term collapse in confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said David Brown, chief European economist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  The ECB does not target exchange rates and goes out of its way to point this out at every opportunity. But Duisenberg, echoing the tone adopted by the Bundesbank before him, has made clear the ECB does not like excessive volatility or rates which are too far out of line with economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Brown argued that by talking tough and backing it with action the ECB could shift market sentiment lastingly in the euro's favour - and do itself a favour in the process. \par \par \uc2  "This week is a high noon for the ECB...and the markets want to see the colour of the ECB's money," he said. \par \par \uc2  But other economists were sceptical whether any ECB action could do much good given that the roots of the euro's problems lay outside the ECB's sphere of influence. \par \par \uc2  "What is hurting the euro is concern about about government structural policies and there's little the ECB can do here," Eckhard Schulte of Industrial Bank of Japan in Frankfurt said. \par \par \uc2  But the ECB could at least redouble its efforts to avoid confusing markets with its own statements, he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted that, unlike in July when the euro's slide towards the dollar parity triggered a cacophony of conflicting statements, this time around the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared much more composed and confident. \par \par \uc2  The overall message that there was no major cause for concern had come across. \par \par \uc2  ((Frankfurt Newsroom +49 69 756525, frankfurt.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbt093t7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
