\par\par \b \uc2  WORLD BONDS-Yen gains pose challenge to investors. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jeffrey Hodgson \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 15:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 29 (Reuters) - The yen's surge against the dollar and euro is having major implications for global bond investors as it has left them even further underweight Japan relative to major indices, analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Global bond investors are being forced to decide whether to buy Japanese government bonds (JGBs) simply to maintain their previous position on the market, or take a more bearish stance on the market by doing nothing. \par \par \uc2  "International managers who are short the Japan element of the index, which is a great many, are suffering a double whammy," said Matt King, fixed-income strategist with J.P. Morgan. \par \par \uc2  "On the one hand you have Japan issuing like crazy, which means that you're getting shorter and shorter by not owning it. And on the other hand if you have the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthening, then it becomes a larger proportion of the overall index." \par \par \uc2  Indices are the benchmark by which money managers are judged and influence how they structure their portfolios. If managers assemble their portfolios out of line with the index it increases the chance that they could lag or outperform the industry norm. So far this year major government bond indicies have fallen. \par \par \uc2  Japan's share of global bond market indices has swelled in the past 12 months through a combination of factors. \par \par \uc2  While other major issuers such as the United States have run budget surpluses, Japan has been borrowing heavily to ensure an economic recovery. At the same time a surge in the yen to a four-year high against the dollar has given Japan a bigger share of dollar-denominated indices. \par \par \uc2  Salomon's world government bond index has seen its Japanese component rise from 19.8 percent in January to 25.45 percent in November. The JGB component of Merrill Lynch's global government bond index has grown to 22.57 percent on November 25 from 17.72 percent on December 31 of last year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said this has resulted in some modest buying of JGBs by managers who do not wish to fall grossly out of line with major bonds indices. \par \par \uc2  But analysts were divided on the wisdom of money managers rushing to add JGBs to their portfolios. The deciding factor for many is their outlook on whether the yen will continue to climb. \par \par \uc2  "It looks as if the market is set on taking the yen at least to 100 (yen per dollar) in the short term, and then seeing from there," said Richard Sherwin, a Japanese bond specialist with Salomon Smith Barney in London. \par \par \uc2  "If we manage to break through, that could be a trigger point for a lot of funds to start to purchase JGBs just to reduce their risk," he said. \par \par \uc2  But other analysts point to potential stumbling blocks for Japanese debt markets. One is the prospect of a pick-up in economic growth sending yields higher. Another is the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement, likely in December, of it's supply requirements for the next fiscal year. Last year's announcement sent prices sliding. \par \par \uc2  And many market watchers are sceptical about the durability of the yen's rise after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on Monday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again. \par \par \uc2  "The whole idea of being underweight the index isn't necessarily a bad thing if you share our pessimism that there's a lot of bad news that's probably on the way for the JGB market," said Joanne Collins, senior economist with Daiwa SBCM in London. \par \par \uc2  "We could see yields break 2.0 percent and possible 2.25 percent before we get too far into next year". \par \par \uc2  In the longer term, analysts said high deficits mean Japan's share of indices will continue to grow, while low yields relative to other markets will discourage many international investors. \par \par \uc2  J.P. Morgan's King said this could lead to fund managers adopting indices which do not include Japan. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, 44 171 542 8663, fax 44 171 542 5285, uk.governmentbonds.news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbt092x1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
