\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Usui: MOF To Take Decisive  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Steps If Needed \b0\par\par\uc2 259 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 08:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The top bureaucrat at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday that the ministry will take decisive steps against a sharp rise in the yen if necessary. \par \par \uc2  "The ministry is carefully watching the market situation and will take decisive measures if needed," said Administrative Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui in a regular press conference. \par \par \uc2  He also said that the Bank of Japan took proper steps Monday to curb a sharp rise in the yen, as a rapid surge in the yen's value would have negative impact on the Japanese economy. However, he wouldn't comment on specific levels of the yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  When asked whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro/yen market, Usui declined to make any specific comments. \par \par \uc2  "I will refrain from making any comment on the details of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  As for coordination with other nations, Usui reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy mentioned in the communique issued by the Group of Seven leading industrial nations in September is unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "In the G7 communique, it was stated that we would continue watching \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and take proper action. There is no change from that," Usui said. \par \par \uc2  Regarding a possible recommendation of former Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara to the position of International Monetary Fund Managing Director, Usui said that while MOF considers Sakakibara suitable for the position, "the government hasn't officially decided to make a recommendation to the IMF." \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbt0841x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
