\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT:Kuroda -2: Usui Also Warns On Yen Over Weekend \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hidenobu Kageyama \par \uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 1999\par 10:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The yen's rise over the last week has been "abnormal" and out of line with economic fundamentals, top officials at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, Japanese vice finance minister for international affairs, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview Sunday that the ministry will take steps to bring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates back into line as needed. \par \par \uc2  "During the last week, especially last Friday, the yen's moves were abnormal," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  "We will appropriately deal with (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) while watching developments carefully," Kuroda added. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda was speaking after the dollar fell to its lowest level versus the yen in nearly four years during New York trading Friday. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as low as Y101.25 during the session - its lowest level since Dec. 21, 1995 - before rebounding to around Y101.60 at the end of trading. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's remarks underscored growing concern among Japanese financial authorities that the strong yen could knock the wind out of the nascent economic recovery by undermining exporter earnings. The strong yen reduces the value of overseas revenue when repatriated into the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Kuroda was joined by Administrative Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui in warning about the strong yen over the weekend. Usui told the Nihon Keizai Shimbun (Nikkei) Saturday that the ministry's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Usui said: "We will make up our minds in the next week. There is no change in the fact that we are ready to take decisive action" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, according to a report in the Sunday morning edition of the Nikkei. \par \par \uc2  Japanese financial authorities haven't entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since mid-summer, when dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to keep the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from dropping below Y120. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo traders said the dollar will likely drop below Y100.00 in short order unless the ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "I think, amid market expectations that the Japanese economy will eventually recover soon, foreign investors such as U.S. pension funds started actively buying the yen," said Kazuki Fujita, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at the Industrial Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Fujita added: "If the MOF won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar will fall below Y100." \par \par \uc2  -By Hidenobu Kageyama (813-5255-2929;hidenobu.kageyama@ dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvbs087nk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
