\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Miyazawa indicates growing concern over yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 1999\par 11:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa indicated Japan's growing concern over the yen's rise on Friday, especially against the sagging euro, saying Tokyo was ready to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials for months have said they are ready to act to curb the yen's rise and in recent days have fretted over the euro's falls to lifetime lows, but Miyazawa accentuated the threat that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  might sell yen in the market. \par \par \uc2  "I told the officials in charge to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they needed to," Miyazawa told reporters late on Friday, adding that they had not yet done so, Jiji news agency reported. \par \par \uc2  The dollar on Friday slipped below 104 yen, hitting three-week lows and entering a zone that markets think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keen to defend. The dollar poked back above the 104-yen line after Miyazawa spoke. \par \par \uc2  But the ministry has signalled this week that the euro is its main concern. The euro slid to a record low versus the yen at 105.55 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy, Haruhiko Kuroda, told reporters on Friday morning: "The euro, in particular, is too weak. It has a lot of room to rise against both the yen and the dollar." \par \par \uc2  But Kuroda, the vice minister for international affairs, did not take the opportunity to give the markets an explicit warning, saying only: "On \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, there is no change in our position." \par \par \uc2  Ministry officials since June - when the yen began a sharp ascent on hopes of a Japanese economic recovery - have said their policy is that a rapid yen rise is unfavourable and that they will respond as appropriate. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 18 through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying euros for yen, but during the latest euro fall the markets have strongly doubted that the ECB would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own behalf to defend the yen. \par \par \uc2  ((William Mallard/Tokyo newsroom +813-3432-8018 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbq099h8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
