\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW - BOJ could use unsterilised  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Eriko Amaha \par \uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 1999\par 12:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 25 (Reuters) - Unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be one option that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could use to stem an unwelcome appreciation of the yen, Flemming Larsen, deputy director of the IMF's Research Department, said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "If there is reason for concern that (the yen's rise) may threaten to exacerbate deflationary pressures and put at risk the recovery that now is finally beginning to be seen, unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be one way to deal with this problem," Larsen told Reuters Television in an interview. \par \par \uc2  Central banks normally "sterilise" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mopping up the money they have pumped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, thereby neutralising the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effects on money supply. \par \par \uc2  By leaving yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilised, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would expand the money supply and in theory put further downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Larsen said any significant appreciation in the yen's value before Japan's economic recovery becomes much firmer would be undesirable. \par \par \uc2  Asked about suggestions that Tokyo's monetary authorities should pursue a policy of inflation targeting, Larsen said it might be a useful compliment to the monetary framework, but added that mild deflation has not become a major problem for Japan. \par \par \uc2  "If the downward pressure on prices were to continue, the case for an inflation-target approach might become stronger. I think it is an option that needs to remain on their consideration," Larsen said. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a cure-all for Japan's problems, but it is a device that might be useful," he added. On U.S. monetary policy, Larsen said the Federal Reserve may need to raise interest rates further if the U.S. economy does not slow down over the first few months of next year. \par \par \uc2  Larsen said there were many indications that the strength of domestic demand in the United States was not sustainable, such as a tight labour market and widening balance of payments deficits. \par \par \uc2  The Fed last raised its key short-term interest rates just a week ago by 25 basis points. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbp097m5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
