\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Euro driven to fresh lifetime low on yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 1999\par 08:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The euro sank to a new lifetime low against the yen of 106.20 yen on Thursday in Tokyo trading, dragged down by growing doubts over Europe's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors were rumoured to be active sellers for today, but overseas speculators were also looking for chances to drive down the euro," a Japanese trust bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  The euro later recovered somewhat against the yen to end the Tokyo session at around 106.57, propped up by speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come to its rescue. \par \par \uc2  Bbut dealers said the struggling euro looked set to surrender more ground. \par \par \uc2  "The euro will be under pressure until the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the downtrend, but until that, the market will likely remain eager about testing the euro below parity in the near term," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  UNDER ATTACK ON TWO FRONTS \par \par \uc2  The euro has come under attack on two fronts - a belief Europe's economic reforms are moving too slowly and an emerging view that Japan looks set for a healthy rebound. \par \par \uc2  "It appears that the market is overly pessimistic about the economic outlook in the euro zone. The market is very keen to react to negative news on the euro, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries are also growing, both in euro/yen and dollar/yen," a Japanese commercial bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The euro has lost about 21 percent against the yen since its launch on January 4. In New York on Wednesday it crashed to fresh four-month lows against the dollar at $1.0156 - not far from a record low $1.0108 set July 13. \par \par \uc2  Late in a tense session in Tokyo on Thursday the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at $1.0195, against $1.0280 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  FEARS OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars against the strong yen also limited aggressive follow-up sales in the euro against the yen, the dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In thin trading ahead of the U.S. Thanksgiving Day holiday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed to a morning high of around 105 yen where it met with resistance that eventually drove it back to 104.48/55 by late in the Tokyo session. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda warned that the euro was now "too weak" and should snap back and rise against the yen. \par \par \uc2  When asked his opinion of the euro's recent downtrend, Kuroda told reporters: "The euro is too weak. It (the euro) is undervalued right now, and it should rise against the yen and the dollar in the future." \par \par \uc2  Traders in Tokyo said some Japanese banks and life insurers were rumoured to be looking to sell European assets given rapid falls of the euro and bond prices. \par \par \uc2  Pessimism towards European assets gathered steam following a report that U.S.-German auto giant DaimlerChrysler AG would rather take a stake in Japan's Honda Motor Co than in Fiat SpA of Italy or PSA Peugeot Citroen of France. \par \par \uc2  The report, in an edition of weekly German business magazine Wirtschaftswoche, reinforced fears that European assets are losing their appeal amid signs of economic weakness in the 11-nation euro zone and fears of a slowing in structural reforms. \par \par \uc2  Honda said on Thursday it is not considering an equity tie-up with DaimlerChrysler, while DaimlerChrysler declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  Weaker economic data from Germany, the euro zone's largest economy, has put a dampener on hopes for recovery, while high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by European governments into the private sector have made some investors doubt their commitment to much-needed structural reform. \par \par \uc2  Among the latter were Germany's recent attempts to rescue construction firm Philipp Holzmann AG  and to block a bid for Mannesmann AG  by Britain's Vodafone AirTouch Plc . \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbp096l1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
