\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  options market sees strong yen, wary of BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 1999\par 13:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 10 (Reuters) - Implied volatility in dollar/yen options rose on Wednesday ahead of Japan's expected announcement of a yen-positive economic package, and volatility was seen rising further. \par \par \uc2  Japanese economic ministers are to hold a special meeting at 2330 GMT on Wednesday to approve the package. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Wednesday he thought the size of the package would be close to last year's package worth 17 trillion yen, above earlier market expectations of around 15 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  While the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market expects a large fiscal package to strengthen the yen, fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation was likely to counterbalance those expectations and keep options volatility high, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Volatilities in the front end have risen a bit and should continue to rise towards tomorrow," said Alexander Nagel, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "If the package is around 16 trillion yen or even above, this could boost the yen and implied volatilities as well, as the market feels the BOJ (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 103." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Spot dollar/yen\tab   was trading around 105 at 1050 GMT, while one-week volatility\tab     was quoted at 12.90/13.90 percent and one-month at 12.80/13.20 percent, slightly above volatility levels seen in Chicago on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The one-month/one-year volatility spread was at 1.8 percent, having narrowed more than half a point this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But there was decline in the premium which investors are willing to pay for options giving them the right to buy rather than sell yen for dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One-month 25 delta risk reversals were at 2.55/2.85 in favour of yen calls, from around 3.0 in Chicago on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Near-term, the risk reversals are favouring yen calls, but we are seeing dollar-call buying on a six-month view," said Mark Henry, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at GNI in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was little action in euro/dollar\tab     implied volatilities, with both spot and volatility likely to be rangebound ahead of the Federal Open Market Committee meeting on November 16, analysts said.\par Sixteen out of 30 top Wall Street brokerages expected no change in rates, a Reuters survey showed. The federal funds target rate is currently at 5.25 percent. One-month euro/dollar volatility was trading at 9.45/9.85 percent and one-year at 10.20/10.45 percent, slightly softer than in Chicago on Tuesday. Spot euro/dollar\tab   was trading around $1.04.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Carolyn Cohn, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvba006kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
