\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan warns strong yen poses risk for growth. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 1999\par 19:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 9 (Reuters) - Haruhiko Kuroda, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said on Tuesday recent yen strength was not appropriate and Japan could not afford to relax its efforts to keep economic recovery on track. \par \par \uc2  Bankers who attended a private meeting with Kuroda at the Bank of England told Reuters he had said that while the Japanese economy was past its worst, the recovery was not as firmly established as had been hoped for. \par \par \uc2  "A rapid and sharp appreciation of the yen at this point would seriously undermine future growth prospects," Kuroda said according to the text of the speech made available to the bankers, which was later obtained by Reuters. \par \par \uc2  In reply to a question, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said that while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the foreign exchanges, any such action would be a strategic decision and would not be decided beforehand, bankers who attended the meeting said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen at least 10 times since June. At least five of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  have taken place since Kuroda took up his job in July, replacing Eisuke Sakakibara, the man dubbed Mr. Yen for his influence over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen as much as 13 percent since the first June \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hitting three-and-a-half-year lows around 103.20 in September. It was trading around 105 yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's comments on the undesireability of yen strength prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers to sell yen but the dollar quickly ceded gains of about a quarter of a yen after his later comments assuaged concern about the risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  SENTIMENT OF MORE CONCERN THAN EXPORTS \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said the authorities were more concerned that a rapid appreciation of the yen could dent confidence than about the impact such a move would have on exports, bankers who attended the meeting said. \par \par \uc2  He therefore stressed the need to ensure the economic recovery was not aborted. \par \par \uc2  "Japan should not, indeed cannot afford to be complacent about its prospects for recovery given that the recent economic indicators are mixed even though they show an undeniable improvement overall," he said in the text of his speech. \par \par \uc2  He noted that while public works spending was rising and housing construction was strong, helped by government incentives, capital investment was still on a declining trend. \par \par \uc2  His comments come on the same day that Japan's Economic Planning Agency revised down growth figures for the second quarter to 0.1 percent from a preliminary estimate of 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Still, the EPA said it was still confident of achieving a target of 0.5 percent growth for the fiscal year to March 2000. \par \par \uc2  Bankers at the meeting said Kuroda was also modestly optimistic about Japan's economic outlook, but that he had been cautious about the near-term prospects. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said in the text of the speech that economic recovery was Japan's priority and that fiscal consolidation would follow later. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government is expected to announce details of a fiscal stimulus package worth about 15 trillion yen later this week. EPA chief Taichi Sakaiya said on Tuesday seven trillion to eight trillion worth of new government bonds could be issued to pay for the supplementary budget. \par \par \uc2  ((Swaha Pattanaik, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvb900dkt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
