\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  volume falls but govt buying helps. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 1999\par 06:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 8 (Reuters) - A decline in capital outflows, fewer leveraged speculative dealings and a drive to cut costs have resulted in a sharp fall in Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volumes so far this year, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts and dealers say. \par \par \uc2  While large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities and genuine demand from overseas investors have picked up some of the slack, they say the usual year-end slowdown in trade is kicking in earlier than usual this year because of concern over possible computer disruptions from the year 2000 date change. \par \par \uc2  Turnover for spot dollar/yen trade amounted to $2.40 trillion between January and October, compared with $3.00 trillion for the same period last year, according to data from the Money Brokers' Association of Japan. \par \par \uc2  The volume of swap dollar/yen transactions fell to $1.87 trillion from $2.25 trillion. \par \par \uc2  Japan is still the third-busiest market for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  after London and New York, but trading volumes have been falling for several years. \par \par \uc2  "Last year, trade was driven by speculative operators pushing up the dollar and by Japanese investors actively investing abroad. This year, speculative dealing has tailed off considerably and we're seeing a market driven by real money from overseas and by real demand," said Mitsumaru Kumagai, a senior economist in the investment research department at Industrial Bank of Japan (IBJ). \par \par \uc2  Net overseas investment in Japanese stocks amounted to 7.39 trillion yen in the first eight months of the year, dwarfing the total of 948.1 billion yen for the same period in 1998, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. \par \par \uc2  Acquisitions of Japanese companies by foreign corporations and the sale of overseas assets by Japanese firms have also propped up \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volumes. \par \par \uc2  BANKS REDUCE RISKS, FIRMS TRY TO CUT COSTS \par \par \uc2  For example, Renault SA financed its purchase of a 36.8 percent stake in Nissan Motor in March for 643 billion yen through debt and cash, selling euros for yen. \par \par \uc2  "What characterised this year was overseas direct and capital investment into Japanese assets," said Takehiro Sato, an economist at Morgan Stanley Dean Witter Japan. \par \par \uc2  By contrast, efforts by Japanese financial institutions to shore up their balance sheets have reduced their ability to take risks, while the yen's rise against the dollar and the euro has dampened interest in investing abroad. \par \par \uc2  A wave of consolidation in the banking industry and an emphasis by Japanese firms on cutting transaction costs have also contributed to falling volumes. \par \par \uc2  For example, Japanese exporters and importers have been attempting to reduce transaction costs by "netting" their dollar-based obligations, Sato said. \par \par \uc2  Monthly trading peaked at $673.4 billion in March due to fiscal year-end settlements and would have been even more lacklustre without the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively to stem the yen's rise against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Acting as the government's agent, the BOJ bought an estimated $25.65 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in June - boosting volume for the month to $594.0 billion - and an estimated $21.82 billion in July and September. \par \par \uc2  TRADE TO SLOW AS Y2K LOOMS \par \par \uc2  Volume is already slowing sharply as dealers reduce exposure in case older computer systems fail to cope with the date change to the year 2000 (Y2K). \par \par \uc2  Risk-averse players are declining to act as counterparties for swaps transactions that cover the year-end, dealers say. \par \par \uc2  "There were indications that dealers were already closing positions during October. Trading volumes will probably fall at a faster pace than in normal years," said Koji Fukaya, chief analyst of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  While trade should pick up after Y2K-related jitters subside, Morgan Stanley's Sato believes turnover in the Tokyo market will continue to lag as long as expectations linger that the yen could climb further. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors can't actively increase their foreign holdings until a bottom emerges for the dollar against the yen," he said. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvb800gbs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
