\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr eases in early Asia on Japan stimulus report. \b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 1999\par 00:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 4 (Reuters) - The dollar was easier against the yen in early Asian trading on Thursday, dragged down after a major Japanese financial daily reported a planned Japanese economic stimulus package would likely be expanded. Dealers said the dollar was likely to be restrained against the yen due to dollar selling interest among Japanese exporters and yen-buying by foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  But any declines below 104.00 yen were likely to be limited due to the potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar stood at 104.83/88 yen\tab   as of 2310 GMT against 104.90/98 yen in late New York on Wednesday.\par The euro\tab   stood at $1.0481/86 as of 2311 GMT against $1.0482/86 in late New York trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the euro was likely to be confined to a narrow range ahead of a meeting of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governing council later on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nihon Keizai Shimbun newspaper reported on Thursday that an economic stimulus package to be compiled by the Japanese government would likely exceed 15 trillion yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par When the report first reached traders in New York overnight, it triggered a fall in the dollar to below 104 yen, before a wave of active short-covering pushed it back up near 105 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yoshio Suzuki, Liberal Party deputy policy chief, said on Wednesday that policymakers had not decided on the final size of the package, which to be announced around November 10.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi has promised a package of more than 10 trillion yen, and senior government officials have estimated it would be worth some 11 trillion to 13 trillion yen. Dealers said the yen had the upper hand in the near term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The dollar is likely to be pushed down by yen-buying by overseas investors and (dollar) selling interest among Japanese exporters," a trust bank dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A larger than expected stimulus package could improve the outlook for Japan's economy and boost the yen by spurring foreign investment in Japanese assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the market has mostly discounted a 25 basis-point credit-tightening by the ECB.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "European money markets seem to have even discounted a 50 basis point rate hike, so I'm not sure how much the euro would rise even if that happened," the trust bank dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some said the resignation of French Finance Minister Dominique Strauss-Kahn was psychologically negative for the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "He resigned over a political scandal, and it has hurt the euro, partly because the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has shown a tendency to react to negative news recently," the trust bank dealer said. Strauss-Kahn quit on Tuesday after becoming embroiled in a corruption scandal. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvb300qaa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
