\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Down Vs Yen On Japan Stimulus Package Talk \b0\par\par\uc2  By Benjamin Pedley \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 1999\par 06:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar fell against the yen Wednesday in Asia on market speculation of a larger-than- expected Japanese fiscal stimulus package, and expectations that offshore investors' appetite for Japanese stocks will continue. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was barely changed against the euro, but trading in both pairs was limited due to a holiday in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect dollar weakness against the yen to continue, but say declines are unlikely to be rapid due to nervousness over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Y103s. \par \par \uc2  "There is inevitably a lot of talk about Japanese fiscal package around...and there is some talk of a larger package," says David Simmonds, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank Salomon Smith Barney in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar-yen still looks quite heavy at Y104.00 and a run down toward the recent low of 103.20 is conceivable," he says. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo is expected to unveil its next stimulus package on Nov. 9. Speculation over the past week has centered on a package of Y11 trillion, although the dollar rose late Tuesday in Asia on talk that the package would be less than this amount. \par \par \uc2  Simmonds said yen strength likely will be helped by overseas investors' enthusiasm for Japanese equities. \par \par \uc2  This enthusiasm is likely to continue as the economy shows further signs of revival, he says. \par \par \uc2  The only factors likely to change this bearish outlook on the dollar against the yen would be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen, or an aggressive loosening of monetary policy, Simmonds says. \par \par \uc2  While neither appear likely at the moment, the possibility of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in the thoughts of most traders. \par \par \uc2  "While it looks as though the dollar will continue to move lower, I find it difficult to be short under Y104...the BOJ is always in the back of my mind," says a U.S. bank trader in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the BOJ was rumored to have "checked" prices in the Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week at around Y103.75, which heightens the importance of this level. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (12:15 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y104.00, below Y104.22 late Tuesday in New York and Y104.68 late Tuesday in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0525, higher than $1.0520 late Tuesday in New York, and unchanged from $1.0525 in Singapore late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was barely changed ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy meeting Thursday, which most analysts expect will decide to lift interest rates by 0.25 percentage point. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has been very quiet this week, its been trading a 30-point range in Asia with all the moves happening in the Europe and the U.S.," says a trader from a U.S. bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Market participants expect euro trade will be subdued up until the ECB rate decision. \par \par \uc2  While a rate rise could be expected to buoy the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say it could prove to be a case of buy the rumor, sell the fact. For the moment, the euro is well supported on a technical basis at $1.0450, although a break below could shake out a few weak longs from around the market. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y109.50, down from Y110.10 in New York late Tuesday. The euro was at Y110.18 in Singapore late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6470, up from $1.6448 in New York late Tuesday and $1.6441 in Singapore late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Benjamin Pedley; 65 421 4806; benjamin.pedley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The following are the dollar's exchange rates at 0515 GMT (12:15 a.m.\par EST) in Singapore, compared with quotations late Tuesday in New York and\par late Tuesday in Tokyo:\par            Singapore Late   New York Late     Tokyo Late\par            Wednesday        Tuesday           Tuesday\par Yen        103.97-02        104.17-27         104.63-73\par Euro       1.0515-25        1.0517-22         1.0520-30\par Sterling   1.6467-77        1.6443-53         1.6436-46\par S-Franc    1.5264-69        1.5273-83         1.5236-46\par CDN Dlr    1.4677-88        1.4688-98         1.4655-65\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0559GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1034 1863 28038 4162 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvb307rmo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
