\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Euro buffeted as Strauss-Kahn resigns. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 1,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 1999\par 13:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 2 (Reuters) - The euro briefly dipped to session lows against the dollar and weakened against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday on reports - later confirmed - that French Finance Minister Dominique Strauss-Kahn had resigned. \par \par \uc2  The euro slipped as far as $1.0496 from an earlier $1.0556 high as French daily Le Monde leaked the announcement. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly recovered above $1.05 as the resignation was not seen threatening euro zone stability or denting expectation of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate hike this week. \par \par \uc2  Strauss-Kahn, who faces allegations of forgery in a corruption scandal which he has denied, announced he was quitting at a news conference on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "It (the resignation) is not good news for the euro, but the market is quite appropriately assessing its impact," said Stewart Newnham, senior strategist at State Street Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The euro evaluation is determined on the basis of monetary policy, and that is in the hands of the ECB rather than in those of the French Ministry." \par \par \uc2  The euro weakened below 110 yen and set a session low of 63.83 pence against sterling in wake of the resignation before retracing some of the losses. \par \par \uc2  Fabio Fraschetti, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNL in London, said the announcement had given euro/dollar traders the opportunity of taking some profit but would not result in protracted euro weakness. \par \par \uc2  EXPECTATION OF ECB RATE MOVE UNCHANGED \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the euro had risen as far as $1.0556 after a batch of strong euro-zone purchasing managers index and producer prices reports reinforced expectations of a ECB monetary tightening at Thursday's governing council meeting. \par \par \uc2  "This (Strauss-Kahn's resignation) is not going to change the medium-term outlook for inflation in the euro zone and therefore it is not going to change ECB policy," Lorenzo Codogno, European economist at Bank of America in London, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  A leading German paper reported on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sources said the ECB would raise its benchmark refinancing rate by 50 basis points from the current 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  More than 80 percent of economists polled by Reuters on Monday expected the ECB to tighten this week, although they were almost evenly split on the size of a rate move, with 12 predicting a quarter point hike and 14 expecting a half-point move. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England was also widely expected to raise its 5.25-percent repo rate at the monthly Monetary Policy Commetee meeting on Wednesday and Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Prospects of higher rates in Britain were reinforced by a report by mortgage lender Halifax, which showed that UK house prices had soared at levels not seen in a decade. \par \par \uc2  The pound rose toward $1.65 from $1.6440/50 late in Europe on Monday on the news but had settled into a tight range in the mid-$1.64 area by midsession. \par \par \uc2  British Prime Minister Tony Blair said on Tuesday the government's economic criteria for joining the euro were not yet met, but his remarks had little market impact. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was holding in tight ranges below 105 yen, with the market focusing on developments in Europe and trading volume thinning ahead of a market holiday in Japan on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen is drifting along with the euro," said the chief dealer of a Japanese bank in London. "Most of the activity is concentrated on euro/yen rather than on dollar/yen given the European figures and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings." \par \par \uc2  Traders said fear of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was keeping the dollar propped above 104.50 yen, but added that a breach of this support would drag the greenback toward 104. \par \par \uc2  Fabio Fraschetti, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNL in London, said the announcement had given euro/dollar traders the opportunity of taking some profit but would not result in protracted euro weakness. \par \par \uc2  EXPECTATION OF ECB RATE MOVE UNCHANGED \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the euro had risen as far as $1.0556 after a batch of strong euro-zone purchasing managers index and producer prices reports reinforced expectations of a ECB monetary tightening at Thursday's governing council meeting. \par \par \uc2  "This (Strauss-Kahn's resignation) is not going to change the medium-term outlook for inflation in the euro zone and therefore it is not going to change ECB policy," Lorenzo Codogno, European economist at Bank of America in London, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  A leading German paper reported on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sources said the ECB would raise its benchmark refinancing rate by 50 basis points from the current 2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  More than 80 percent of economists polled by Reuters on Monday expected the ECB to tighten this week, although they were almost evenly split on the size of a rate move, with 12 predicting a quarter point hike and 14 expecting a half-point move. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England was also widely expected to raise its 5.25-percent repo rate at the monthly Monetary Policy Commetee meeting on Wednesday and Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Prospects of higher rates in Britain were reinforced by a report by mortgage lender Halifax, which showed that UK house prices had soared at levels not seen in a decade. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The pound\tab   rose toward $1.65 from $1.6440/50 late in Europe on Monday on the news but had settled into a tight range in the mid-$1.64 area by midsession.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par British Prime Minister Tony Blair said on Tuesday the government's economic criteria for joining the euro were not yet met, but his remarks had little market impact.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dollar/yen\tab   was holding in tight ranges below 105 yen, with the market focusing on developments in Europe and trading volume thinning ahead of a market holiday in Japan on Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Dollar/yen is drifting along with the euro," said the chief dealer of a Japanese bank in London. "Most of the activity is concentrated on euro/yen rather than on dollar/yen given the European figures and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meetings."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said fear of possible Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was keeping the dollar propped above 104.50 yen, but added that a breach of this support would drag the greenback toward 104.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((London Capital Markets, 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvb200yzs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
