\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Euro gains on ECB rate prospects. \b0\par\par\uc2 946 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 1999\par 10:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 1 (Reuters) - The euro rose broadly early in Europe on Monday, buoyed by heightened expectation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise interest rates at this week's governing council meeting. \par \par \uc2  Although the market had been anticipating a ECB credit tightening for some time, ECB President Wim Duisenberg's comments in Sunday's Handelsblatt newspaper boosted the prospect of an imminent rate move, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg was quoted as saying the ECB's tightening bias had slightly strengthened since July and that he could "imagine" what the council members could decide on at Thursday's meeting. \par \par \uc2  "It looks as if the ECB is trying to be like the Fed (U.S. Federal Reserve) and flag its moves in advance," said a trader with a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro\tab   was at $1.0577/79 at 0805 GMT against $1.0515/17 late in European trading on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said a ECB tightening this week was now becoming a foregone conclusion after a series of hawkish comments from top ECB policymakers last week reinforced by a jump in euro zone M3 money supply growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "All the rhetoric suggests we will get a change (in interest rates)," Jane Foley, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclays Capital, told Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, she expected that a rate rise would be by 25 basis points and that it would likely provide only a limited boost to the single European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since the market had already discounted such a move.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/yen\tab      jumped nearly one yen to the mid-110-yen area on short-covering as euro/dollar began rising. Traders said many in the market had become short euros and dollars against the yen overnight amid optimism over Japan's economic recovery.\par Dollar/yen\tab   was last at 104.28/33 yen against an overnight low of 103.75. It had bounced from the lows earlier amid wariness of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and later benefited from the euro/yen gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said the market was becoming jittery because Japanese authorities were stepping up their rhetoric about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the dollar neared its 3-1/2 year low around 103.20 yen set in September.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese Finance Ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda said overnight that the recent yen rise was excessive, both in terms of speed and levels. Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui also warned that Japan would take decisive action if needed to block yen's rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The question in everyone's mind is whether or not we would break below 103.20," said Yuji Tashiro, a dealer at Daiwa Europe Bank in London, adding that talk in the market during the Asian market was that there was a huge stop-loss selling order at 103.70 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Looking at the way it's bounced, it seems like a lot of Tokyo traders were short dollars, perhaps hoping that London would come in and push the dollar lower again," he observed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said a ECB tightening this week was now becoming a foregone conclusion after a series of hawkish comments from top ECB policymakers last week reinforced by a jump in euro zone M3 money supply growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "All the rhetoric suggests we will get a change (in interest rates)," Jane Foley, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclays Capital, told Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, she expected that a rate rise would be by 25 basis points and that it would likely provide only a limited boost to the single European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since the market had already discounted such a move.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/yen\tab      jumped nearly one yen to the mid-110-yen area on short-covering as euro/dollar began rising. Traders said many in the market had become short euros and dollars against the yen overnight amid optimism over Japan's economic recovery.\par Dollar/yen\tab   was last at 104.28/33 yen against an overnight low of 103.75. It had bounced from the lows earlier amid wariness of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and later benefited from the euro/yen gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said the market was becoming jittery because Japanese authorities were stepping up their rhetoric about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the dollar neared its 3-1/2 year low around 103.20 yen set in September.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese Finance Ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda said overnight that the recent yen rise was excessive, both in terms of speed and levels. Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui also warned that Japan would take decisive action if needed to block yen's rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The question in everyone's mind is whether or not we would break below 103.20," said Yuji Tashiro, a dealer at Daiwa Europe Bank in London, adding that talk in the market during the Asian market was that there was a huge stop-loss selling order at 103.70 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Looking at the way it's bounced, it seems like a lot of Tokyo traders were short dollars, perhaps hoping that London would come in and push the dollar lower again," he observed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders expected choppy, cautious trading for the remainder of the day due in part to a holiday in many European countries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The economic data calendar for Monday was light, with sterling traders focusing their attention on UK CIPS manufacturing purchasing managers index due out at 0930 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling gained early against the dollar, helped by the euro/dollar rally. The pound was last at $1.6460/70 against $1.6441/46 late on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. National Association of Purchasing Management index was due out at 1500 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvb1010eo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
