\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Euro perks up, ECB meeting focus. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 1999\par 13:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 1 (Reuters) - The euro was firmer across the board on Monday, supported by growing expectation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise interest rates at this week's governing council meeting. \par \par \uc2  Even though perception the market had already priced in higher ECB rates had depressed the euro over the last few weeks to Friday's one-month low of $1.0431, ECB President Wim Duisenberg's comments in Sunday's Handelsblatt newspaper made the market wary of getting caught short in euros, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg was quoted as saying the ECB's tightening bias had slightly strengthened since July and that he could "imagine" what the council members could decide on at Thursday's meeting. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously, the market has found the comments from Mr Duisenberg...somewhat supportive," said Jeremy Stretch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Global Financial Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.0576/81 at 1112 GMT after rising as high as $1.0593 earlier from $1.0515/17 late in Friday's European trading. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was still sceptical how much the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should gain from a long-anticipated ECB rate move, noting that the euro has so far been hesitant to climb above $1.06. \par \par \uc2  "We've got a lot of Europe on holiday today, so there's not a great deal of follow through," Stretch observed. \par \par \uc2  With little economic data on the agenda for Monday, activity was largely confined to position-squaring ahead of the ECB meeting and of purchasing managers indices for Germany, France, Italy and the euro zone on Tuesday, traders said. Traders said another big question mark over the euro was the fragile U.S. asset markets, with Friday's October U.S. employment report keeping operators nervous. \par \par \uc2  Brokerage firm Salomon Smith Barney estimated on Friday that the jobs data would show a rise of 525,000 in non-farm payroll, well above Wall Street's average forecast of a 313,000 rise, citing effects from Hurricane Floyd, which had caused an 8,000 decline in September payrolls. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. stocks have begun to look a little better in recent sessions, but it's not going to take much for them to turn ugly again," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was helped by euro/dollar's gains and a rise in the CIPS October PMI data, which showed that the manufacturing sector expanded for a fifth straight month, with the index rising to 54.2 from September's 54.1. \par \par \uc2  The data was seen increasing the chance that the Bank of England would raise rates at this week's policy meeting. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was last at $1.6482/87, up from $1.6441/46 late in Europe on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The gains in euro/dollar also prompted some short-covering in the euro/yen market, where short positions had built up due to widespread optimism on Japan's economy. Euro/yen was last at 110.47/57 yen against an overnight trough of 109.52. \par \par \uc2  Yen liquidation spilt over to dollar/yen, particularly with wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  growing as the dollar neared the 3-1/2 year low of 103.17 yen set in September. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda said overnight that the recent yen rise was excessive, both in terms of speed and levels. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen stood at 104.41/50 yen against a session low of 103.75 hit overnight. \par \par \uc2  Minutes of the September 21 BOJ board meeting showed that BOJ policymakers had voted 7-2 in favour of keeping policy steady, 8-1 against easing credit further. \par \par \uc2  The board's lone easing proponent Nobuyuki Nakahara said on Monday the BOJ may have to buy more Japanese government bonds to keep long-term yields from rising. He also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should take quantitative easing measures and consider supplying funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps market. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was still sceptical how much the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should gain from a long-anticipated ECB rate move, noting that the euro has so far been hesitant to climb above $1.06. \par \par \uc2  "We've got a lot of Europe on holiday today, so there's not a great deal of follow through," Stretch observed. \par \par \uc2  With little economic data on the agenda for Monday, activity was largely confined to position-squaring ahead of the ECB meeting and of purchasing managers indices for Germany, France, Italy and the euro zone on Tuesday, traders said. Traders said another big question mark over the euro was the fragile U.S. asset markets, with Friday's October U.S. employment report keeping operators nervous. \par \par \uc2  Brokerage firm Salomon Smith Barney estimated on Friday that the jobs data would show a rise of 525,000 in non-farm payroll, well above Wall Street's average forecast of a 313,000 rise, citing effects from Hurricane Floyd, which had caused an 8,000 decline in September payrolls. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. stocks have begun to look a little better in recent sessions, but it's not going to take much for them to turn ugly again," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was helped by euro/dollar's gains and a rise in the CIPS October PMI data, which showed that the manufacturing sector expanded for a fifth straight month, with the index rising to 54.2 from September's 54.1. \par \par \uc2  The data was seen increasing the chance that the Bank of England would raise rates at this week's policy meeting. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling\tab   was last at $1.6482/87, up from $1.6441/46 late in Europe on Friday.\par The gains in euro/dollar also prompted some short-covering in the euro/yen market, where short positions had built up due to widespread optimism on Japan's economy. Euro/yen\tab      was last at 110.47/57 yen against an overnight trough of 109.52.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yen liquidation spilt over to dollar/yen, particularly with wariness of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  growing as the dollar neared the 3-1/2 year low of 103.17 yen set in September.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese Finance Ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda said overnight that the recent yen rise was excessive, both in terms of speed and levels.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dollar/yen\tab   stood at 104.41/50 yen against a session low of 103.75 hit overnight.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Minutes of the September 21 BOJ board meeting showed that BOJ policymakers had voted 7-2 in favour of keeping policy steady, 8-1 against easing credit further.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The board's lone easing proponent Nobuyuki Nakahara said on Monday the BOJ may have to buy more Japanese government bonds to keep long-term yields from rising. He also said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should take quantitative easing measures and consider supplying funds to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000