\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Euro broadly bolstered by French data. \b0\par\par\uc2 837 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 1999\par 10:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LONDON, Oct 29 (Reuters) - The euro\tab   pushed up against the dollar and yen in early Europe trading on Friday, bolstered by a sharp fall in the French unemployment rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It firmed towards the mid-$1.05s, up about a cent from the one-month low of $1.0468 hit on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "The (French) data were very good. It's very rare to see such a large scale monthly drop in unemployment in France," said Audrey Childe-Freeman, European economist at CIBC World Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "This is very helpful for the euro. People are quickly going to start thinking about next week European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy meeting. We have already enough data and enough comments from ECB members to conclude that they are going to put rates up next week." \par \par \uc2  French unemployment fell by 83,000 or three percent in September, forcing the jobless rate down to 11.1 percent from 11.3 percent in August. \par \par \uc2  The French data offset the support the dollar had drawn from Thursday's 2.19 percent rise in the Dow Jones Industrial Average. It also lifted the euro against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Euro/dollar should test lower today because of the rally in the U.S. equity market, but it doesn't because the market sees this as an opportunity to buy euros at attractive levels," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  Chartists said the euro had to overcome a technical barrier at $1.0560 to extend its gains up to the $1.06 area. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/yen was at 110.70/75\tab      at 0730 GMT against 110.36/54 late in Europe on Thursday.\par Meanwhile the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held above 105 yen\tab  , helped by fears \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . These were kept alive when Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Toshio Miki said overnight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be used to counteract a rapid rise in the yen even if its impact would be limited.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It (Miki's comments) highlights that Japanese officials are worried about yen strength and that they are willing to act on it if necessary," said Dannesboe.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Miki also said the dollar at around 110 yen would be acceptable.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was at 105.03/08 yen against its Asian low of 104.74 and compared with 104.93/98 late in Europe on Thursday\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dollar gains were however capped as the yen was supported by strong gains in Japanese stocks and the announcement overnight of a surprise fall in Japanese unemployment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo's Nikkei stock index closed more than three percent up on Friday, and new data showed the Japanese jobless rate dipped for the second straight month to 4.6 percent in September.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar lost ground overnight after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said the U.S. economy was still growing fast but warned about possible inflation threats posed by the tightness of the labour market. Meanwhile, sterling was stabilising half a cent above its five-week low near $1.6340\tab   hit on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The pound was propped by a report from the National Institute of Economic and Social Research, which said the UK economy would expand at a faster-than-expected 1.8 percent this year and 2.9 percent in 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Independent researcher GfK said that Britain's consumer confidence remained firm in October despite the September UK rate hike.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The French data offset the support the dollar had drawn from Thursday's 2.19 percent rise in the Dow Jones Industrial Average. It also lifted the euro against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Euro/dollar should test lower today because of the rally in the U.S. equity market, but it doesn't because the market sees this as an opportunity to buy euros at attractive levels," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN AMRO in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chartists said the euro had to overcome a technical barrier at $1.0560 to extend its gains up to the $1.06 area.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/yen was at 110.70/75\tab      at 0730 GMT against 110.36/54 late in Europe on Thursday.\par Meanwhile the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held above 105 yen\tab  , helped by fears \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . These were kept alive when Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Toshio Miki said overnight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be used to counteract a rapid rise in the yen even if its impact would be limited.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It (Miki's comments) highlights that Japanese officials are worried about yen strength and that they are willing to act on it if necessary," said Dannesboe.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Miki also said the dollar at around 110 yen would be acceptable.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was at 105.03/08 yen against its Asian low of 104.74 and 