\par\par \b \uc2  ADBI Yoshitomi: Y110-Y115 "Appropriate" Range \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1999\par 06:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese government should shift from a loose fiscal policy to a neutral stance as soon as the economic recovery is deemed self-sustaining, said Masaru Yoshitomi, dean of the Asian Development Bank Institute. \par \par \uc2  Referring to the large economic stimulus package currently being compiled by the government, Yoshitomi told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview, "Fiscal policy should turn neutral once the recovery of Japan's economy is sustainable, because of the nation's large fiscal deficit." \par \par \uc2  A former director-general of Japan's Economic Planning Agency, Yoshitomi warned that increased fiscal spending could push up long-term interest rates and drive up the yen against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "More fiscal stimulus will have a 'crowding out' effect, that is, the yen will appreciate (against the dollar) and rates in the long end will become higher," Yoshitomi said, adding "These two factors will erase most of the stimulative effects" that would result from fiscal spending. \par \par \uc2  To ward off the negative impact of excessive yen strength, he said that Japanese monetary authorities such as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, selling yen for dollars in a "\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate targeting" process. \par \par \uc2  Yoshitomi noted that many politicians have urged the BOJ to adopt quantitative targets recently - such as for inflation or money supply growth - as a means of easing monetary conditions. He said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were a better tool for that type of action. \par \par \uc2  "If the BOJ can target Y110, it may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  a lot. But this is a much clearer target for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shoot for" than other types of targets. \par \par \uc2  "It would also be a sort of quantitative easing via the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate," Yoshitomi said. \par \par \uc2  Yoshitomi expressed concerns over the current level of the yen against the dollar, saying "The yen is too strong against the dollar, given the nation's current business cycle. It should be between Y110 and Y115." \par \par \uc2  The dollar is currently trading around Y104.03, down from Y104.81 in New York late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  He added that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ would help keep \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy on the path toward recovery, as an excessively strong yen would undermine Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  When asked whether solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ would be sufficient to change the current strengthening trend of the yen against the dollar, Yoshitomi said "It (BOJ) has plenty of bullets." \par \par \uc2  He said that the BOJ could conduct more outright buying of long-dated Japanese government bonds to cap possible rises in long-term interest rates in the future. But he also cautioned that the impact of such a move would only be "temporary." \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvas07lw6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
