\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan warns of 'excess' yen rise, mkts cool. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1999\par 06:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Japan threw down the gauntlet on its resurgent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, saying the yen's rise against the dollar and the euro was "excessive" and warning the markets of action to curb its rise. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, although nervous about the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the yen's overnight rise to one-month highs, nonetheless continued to bid the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up. \par \par \uc2  "Yes, it's excessive," said Haruhiko Kuroda, the vice minister for international affairs at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when asked about the yen's renewed climb. \par \par \uc2  He said this referred to the yen's gains against both the dollar and the euro. \par \par \uc2  G7 YEN CONCERN INTACT, \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  MUM ON \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The dollar approached 3-year lows overnight, trading at a low of 103.71 yen, its weakest level since around last month's statement from the Group of Seven industrial powers that they "shared Japan's concern" about the strong yen and vowed to "cooperate as needed" in the exchange markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro fell to 109.05 yen overnight, its lowest since September 15. In Tokyo on Thursday, the yen was trading at 103.99 to the dollar and 109.30 against the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 statement of concern about the effect of yen appreciation, such as at present, on the Japanese and world economies is still in effect," Kuroda told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said Tokyo's policy of combating rapid or excessive yen rises was "absolutely unchanged" and that "we will respond appropriately." \par \par \uc2  But he and his deputy Zembei Mizoguchi were mum on whether Japan would sell the yen - either alone or with other G7 authorities - through \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We are always cooperating, in contact with, various countries as needed," he said. "As was said in the G7 statement, we will monitor the market and cooperate." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the comments made them wary of aggressively buying the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-selling was helped when Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya was quoted as saying he was not yet concerned about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movements while action would be needed if it broke 100 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  MARKET REAWAKENS TO JAPAN RECOVERY STORY \par \par \uc2  The yen, which had been fairly quiet since just after the September 25 G7 meeting, spiked sharply higher overnight on talk that the Bank of Japan was checking with dealers to see what rates they were offering to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have returned repeatedly to the G7 statement, which was unusual in singling out the yen for concern. \par \par \uc2  G7 finance ministers and central bankers issued the statement after the dollar had sunk some 15 percent against the yen since early June, when the Japanese economy began showing clear signs of pulling out of its worst postwar downturn. \par \par \uc2  Foreign money has poured into yen assets this year, and while Tokyo acknowledges that this basically reflects a welcome "buy-Japan" mentality, they said some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements appeared speculative and they feared a yen surge could wreck the nascent recovery. \par \par \uc2  A strong yen hurts the Japanese economy by shrinking the yen value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings and by rekindling deflationary fears in the economy, where prices have finally begun flattening after a long string of declines. \par \par \uc2  Japan won the G7's verbal support after vowing further government spending to keep the policy-driven recovery alive until the private sector takes over and the Bank of Japan promised to be flexible in implementing its policy of driving short-term interest rates to near zero. \par \par \uc2  But recently, the market has once again focused on the story of recovery in the world's second biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  News on Wednesday that July-September industrial output grew from the previous quarter with prospects for strong November growth - on the back of strong August business activity reported on Friday - caused many economists to say they will raise their forecasts for July-September gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  This raised the prospect that the Japanese economy will have posted surprise growth for three consecutive quarters when July-September GDP is released around the beginning of December. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters survey just last week of 23 economists produced a median forecast for a July-September GDP dip of 0.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvas01746\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
