\par\par \b \uc2  Yen up all round, European  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  the losers. \b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1999\par 08:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 28 (Reuters) - The yen held firm across the board late on Thursday in Tokyo as the market continued to buy into the Japanese recovery story and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across Europe suddenly fell from favour. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen\tab   again tested the water beyond 104.00 to the dollar but local investors were just too wary of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to press the matter, traders said.\par Instead, dealers reported good yen buying against a range of crosses including the euro\tab     , sterling, the Swiss franc and the Australian dollar. They noted the U.S. dollar also gained on all these, which reinforced their belief the U.S. would not join \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to depress the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  By 0615 GMT the dollar was hovering at 104.20/25 yen against a 103.88 low and a 104.24/34 finish in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro\tab   in contrast was a major loser, having sagged to $1.0506/12 on Thursday from $1.0577/80 late on Wednesday in Tokyo and a seven-month high around $1.0911 just two weeks ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It also skidded to 109.54/62 yen from 110.36 in Tokyo on Wednesday, and a month high of 116.46, with traders reporting aggressive selling by U.S. hedge funds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest decline was something of a puzzle to dealers since strong European M3 money supply data on Wednesday appeared to increase the chances of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hiking interest rates at its council meeting next week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It does seem a perverse reaction," said a dealer at a European bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "A lot of the lapse was technical, stop-loss selling but that still doesn't explain why the euro couldn't bounce at all," he said. "There's a suggestion the market's suddenly worried in case a hike derails the recovery there - but we still think higher yields will benefit the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in time."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For the yen, the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trying its best to fan the market's fears on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Both Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda and MOF official Zembei Mizoguchi issued stern verbal warnings against an excessive yen rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par They also tried to resurrect the threat of coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with the G7, but analysts noted that with the dollar actually firming in trade-weighted terms there was scant reason for the U.S. to help the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The MOF's efforts were not helped by a reported comment from Economic Planning Agency Chief Taichi Sakaiya that he was not very concerned with the dollar at 104 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He tempered the remark by saying the government would need to take action if the dollar fell below 100 yen, but it still encouraged sellers to re-test support under 104.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen was also benefiting from a short-term factor in the shape of next month's offer of shares in Japanese telecoms giant Nippon Telegraph and Telephone Corp (NTT) . This is worth more than $13 billion and is attracting heavy investor interest both at home and abroad.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said not only were offshore investors looking to buy yen for the issue but local institutions were thought to be selling some of the foreign telecoms shares in their portfolios in order to raise cash to suscribe.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The issue price for the 952,000 share offer is due to be set between November 5 and 9.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another temptation for would-be yen buyers were the host of options positions believed to lurking at lower levels on both dollar/yen and euro/yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's a big knockout option at 103.70 which a New York bank has been defending and there's another on euro/yen at 109.00 which is attracting some interest," said a U.S. bank dealer.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These options are tempting for speculative sellers, since if they can trigger the knockouts it could entail heavy stop loss buying of yen, so providing them with a healthy profit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The trader added that a lot more options were thought to be layered at 103.20, 103.00, 102.00 and 101.00 yen, so there was the potential for stop loss selling to snowball out of control. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvas0128l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
